  "markdown": "# LIFO Stacks: Stack Frame Managers & Local Scopes\n\nWhile dynamic arrays allow unrestricted random accesses, syntax parsers and call stacks enforce restricted boundary accesses. This restricted protocol is the **LIFO Stack**.\n\nThis chapter explores **LIFO Stacks** (Last-In, First-Out). We focus on memory representation choices (arrays vs. lists), top-bounds offsets, and recursion frame execution paths.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Backing Structures: Array vs. List\n\nA stack can be implemented using either a contiguous dynamic array or a singly linked list.\n\n### Array-Backed Stack\nStores elements contiguously. The top of the stack is tracked using an index index pointer `topIndex`.\n- **Push**: Write to `arr[++topIndex]` ($O(1)$ amortized).\n- **Pop**: Decrement `topIndex` ($O(1)$).\n- **Cache Locality**: High. Sequential memory reads load preloaded cache lines.\n\n### List-Backed Stack\nStores elements in a singly linked list. The top of the stack is the head of the list.\n- **Push**: Prepend a new node to the head of the list ($O(1)$ constant).\n- **Pop**: Remove the head node ($O(1)$ constant).\n- **Cache Locality**: Poor. Node links trigger DRAM lookups and heap allocations.\n\n### Stack Backing Layouts\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Array vs. List Backed Stacks</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Array Backed */}\n    <g>\n      <text x=\"50\" y=\"10\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Array Stack</text>\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"80\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      <rect x=\"5\" y=\"75\" width=\"90\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#1e293b\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\"/>\n      <text x=\"50\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">10</text>\n      <text x=\"120\" y=\"85\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"monospace\">◄ topIndex</text>\n    </g>\n\n    {/* List Backed */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(240, 0)\">\n      <text x=\"60\" y=\"10\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Linked List Stack</text>\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"20\" width=\"120\" height=\"35\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <line x1=\"70\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n      <text x=\"35\" y=\"42\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Val: 10</text>\n      <text x=\"95\" y=\"42\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">null</text>\n      <text x=\"-40\" y=\"42\" fill=\"#f472b6\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"monospace\">Head ──►</text>\n    </g>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Valid Parentheses (LeetCode 20)](https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-parentheses/): Match tokens.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practices tracking matching sets.\n* [Implement Stack using Queues (LeetCode 225)](https://leetcode.com/problems/implement-stack-using-queues/): Re-route queue order.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build queue alignment conversions.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Min Stack (LeetCode 155)](https://leetcode.com/problems/min-stack/): Tracking min values.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practices storing min metadata alongside the main stack frame.\n* [Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation (LeetCode 150)](https://leetcode.com/problems/evaluate-reverse-polish-notation/): Infix/Postfix parser.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Teaches you to parse expression sequences.\n",
