  "slug": "circular-buffers",
  "markdown": "# Circular Buffers: Hardware Rings & Wrap-Around Cursors\n\nIn low-latency systems (such as audio streams, network interface drivers, or logger channels), data arrives as an ongoing stream. Allocating memory dynamically or shifting elements to make space introduces latency spikes. \n\nThis chapter explores **Circular Buffers** (also known as ring buffers). We focus on wrap-around pointer mechanics, synchronization barriers, and memory-mapped virtual ring loops.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Ring Queue Protocol: Write & Read Cursors\n\nA circular buffer wraps a linear array into a logical circle using two pointer index registers:\n1. **Write Cursor (`write`)**: Tracks the next index where new data will be written.\n2. **Read Cursor (`read`)**: Tracks the next index from which data will be consumed.\n\n```\n       [0]  [1]  [2]  [3]\nState:  A    B    C    _\n                 ▲    ▲\n                read write\n```\n\nWhen writing or reading, cursors advance sequentially. When they reach the end of the array, they wrap back to index 0 using modulo arithmetic:\n\n$$\\text{next} = (\\text{curr} + 1) \\bmod \\text{capacity}$$\n\n### Circular Cursor Tracking\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Circular Buffer Cursors</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(60, 60)\">\n    {/* Cells */}\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Val A</text>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Read Pointer</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"50\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Val B</text>\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Write Pointer</text>\n    \n    <circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#374151\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 1\"/>\n    <text x=\"80\" y=\"103\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">_</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Design Circular Queue (LeetCode 622)](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-circular-queue/): Array-based wrap.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build circular index wrap bounds.\n* [Design Circular Deque (LeetCode 641)](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-circular-deque/): Double circular boundaries.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practices wrapping pointers in both directions.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Sliding Window Maximum (LeetCode 239)](https://leetcode.com/problems/sliding-window-maximum/): Rolling window constraints.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Uses circular tracking concepts to manage sliding window statistics.\n",
