  "markdown": "# Max Heaps: Desending Priority Queues & Maximum Extraction\n\nWhile a min heap processes data starting with the smallest values, task schedulers and operating system scheduling queues prioritize high-priority jobs. To extract the maximum element in logarithmic time, we use a **Max Heap**.\n\nThis chapter explores **Max Heaps**. We focus on max-heap properties, bubble-up indexing, and heap sorting.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Max-Heap Property: Parent-Child Invariant\n\nIn a max heap, for every node `i` other than the root:\n\n$$\\text{value}(\\text{parent}(i)) \\ge \\text{value}(i)$$\n\nThe maximum key is always at the root.\n\n```\n       [40]\n      /    \\\n   [30]    [20]\n```\n\n### Max-Heap Tree Structure\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Max Heap Invariant Layout</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"20\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">50 (Root)</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 90,30 L 60,60\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"70\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"73\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">30</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 110,30 L 140,60\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"70\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"73\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">40</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Dynamic Insertion & Sift-Up Paths\n\nWhen you insert a new element:\n1. Append the element to the end of the array.\n2. Sift this element up (**Heapify Up** / **Sift Up**) by comparing it with its parent.\n3. If it is larger than its parent, swap them.\n4. Repeat until the element is smaller than or equal to its parent or reaches the root.\n\n### Heapify Up Insertion\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Heapify Up (Sift Up Path)</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Parent */}\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"20\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">20</text>\n\n    {/* Sift up node */}\n    <path d=\"M 90,30 L 60,60\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"70\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"73\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">45</text>\n\n    {/* Sift-up swap path */}\n    <path d=\"M 60,55 Q 70,40 90,30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-heapify-up)\"/>\n    <text x=\"65\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"monospace\">Swap Up</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-heapify-up\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Last Stone Weight (LeetCode 1046)](https://leetcode.com/problems/last-stone-weight/): Dynamic stone crushing.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build heap updates foundations.\n* [Top K Frequent Elements (LeetCode 347)](https://leetcode.com/problems/top-k-frequent-elements/): Count tracking.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Uses heaps to track frequencies.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Find Median from Data Stream (LeetCode 295)](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-median-from-data-stream/): Dual heap sorting.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Combines a max-heap and a min-heap to keep track of midpoints.\n* [Sliding Window Median (LeetCode 480)](https://leetcode.com/problems/sliding-window-median/): Dual heap window shifts.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Extends dual heap setups to slide window bounds.\n",
