  "markdown": "# Insertion Sort: Online Element Shifting & Sorted Prefix Invariants\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Motivation\n\nWhen sorting files or decks of cards, humans naturally pick one item at a time and insert it into its correct position relative to the already sorted items. In early computing, this intuition was translated into **Insertion Sort**. \n\nWhile advanced algorithms (like Quicksort or Merge Sort) achieve $O(N \\log N)$ average runtimes, they introduce recursion stack and partition allocation overhead.\n\nInsertion Sort operates **in-place** with zero auxiliary space and requires minimal instruction checks. \n\nFor small datasets (typically $N \\le 32$) or datasets that are already nearly sorted, Insertion Sort is faster than Quicksort due to low constant factors and excellent cache locality. Today, it is used as the base case sorting subroutine in hybrid library sorters (such as Python's Timsort and C++'s `std::sort` introsort).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Mathematical Invariants\n\n### The Sorted Prefix Invariant\nAt any step $i$ in Insertion Sort:\n1.  **Sorted Prefix**: The subarray $A[0 \\dots i-1]$ is sorted in ascending order.\n2.  **Unsorted Suffix**: The subarray $A[i \\dots N-1]$ contains the remaining unsorted elements.\n\n---\n\n### Element Shifting Invariant\nTo insert the current key $A[i]$ into the sorted prefix:\n*   We compare the key with elements in the prefix from right to left (index $j = i-1$ down to $0$).\n*   If $A[j] > \\text{key}$, we shift $A[j]$ one position to the right ($A[j+1] = A[j]$) to clear a slot.\n*   We repeat this until we find the correct location, where we insert the key: $A[j+1] = \\text{key}$.\n*   Because we shift elements instead of performing multiple swaps, we reduce memory write cycles.\n\n### Insertion Shifting Pass\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 220\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"220\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"14\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Insertion Sort Element Shifting</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(50, 60)\">\n    {/* Sorted Prefix */}\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"10\" width=\"105\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"52\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Sorted Prefix: [3, 7, 9]</text>\n\n    {/* Key to insert */}\n    <rect x=\"120\" y=\"10\" width=\"30\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#1e1b4b\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"135\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">5</text>\n    <text x=\"135\" y=\"52\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Key</text>\n\n    {/* Shifting visual */}\n    <path d=\"M 100,25 Q 110,5 120,25\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#eab308\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 1\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-insert-shift)\"/>\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"0\" fill=\"#eab308\" font-size=\"7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Compare &amp; Shift 7, 9</text>\n\n    {/* Resulting Array */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(240, 0)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"10\" width=\"145\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <text x=\"72\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">New Prefix: [3, 5, 7, 9]</text>\n    </g>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-insert-shift\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#eab308\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Step-by-Step Execution Trace\n\nLet us trace sorting the array `A = [12, 11, 13, 5, 6]` using Insertion Sort.\n\n### Trace Table\n\n| Pass ($i$) | Key to Insert | Sorted Prefix Bounds | Unsorted Suffix | Comparisons Required | Shift Actions | Resulting Array State |\n| :---: | :---: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | $11$ | `[12]` | `[13, 5, 6]` | $1$ | Shift $12$ right. | `[11, 12, 13, 5, 6]` |\n| **2** | $13$ | `[11, 12]` | `[5, 6]` | $1$ | No shifts needed (inserts at index 2). | `[11, 12, 13, 5, 6]` |\n| **3** | $5$ | `[11, 12, 13]` | `[6]` | $3$ | Shift $13, 12, 11$ right. | `[5, 11, 12, 13, 6]` |\n| **4** | $6$ | `[5, 11, 12, 13]`| Empty | $3$ | Shift $13, 12, 11$ right. | `[5, 6, 11, 12, 13]` |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Production-Ready Implementations\n\n### C++ Source Code\n```cpp\n#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass InsertionSort {\npublic:\n    void sort(vector<int>& arr) {\n        int n = arr.size();\n        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n            int key = arr[i];\n            int j = i - 1;\n\n            // Move elements of arr[0..i-1], that are greater than key,\n            // to one position ahead of their current position\n            while (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {\n                arr[j + 1] = arr[j]; // Shift element right\n                j--;\n            }\n            arr[j + 1] = key; // Insert key at correct position\n        }\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### Python Source Code\n```python\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass InsertionSort:\n    def sort(self, arr: List[int]):\n        n = len(arr)\n        for i in range(1, n):\n            key = arr[i]\n            j = i - 1\n            \n            # Move elements of arr[0..i-1], that are greater than key,\n            # to one position ahead of their current position\n            while j >= 0 and arr[j] > key:\n                arr[j + 1] = arr[j]  # Shift element right\n                j -= 1\n            arr[j + 1] = key  # Insert key at correct position\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Asymptotic Time & Space Complexity\n\n*   **Time Complexity**:\n    *   **Worst Case**: $O(N^2)$ when the array is sorted in reverse order.\n    *   **Best Case**: $O(N)$ when the array is already sorted, executing only 1 comparison per pass.\n*   **Space Complexity**: $O(1)$ auxiliary space.\n*   **Stability**: **Stable**. Duplicate elements preserve their relative order because the shift condition is strictly greater than (`arr[j] > key`), meaning equal elements do not trigger shifts.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Systems Connections: Timsort & Introsort Subroutine\n\nIn system sorting libraries:\n*   **Python standard sorter (Timsort)**: Timsort partitions arrays into runs, sorts small runs (typically size $\\le 32$) using Insertion Sort, and merges them using Merge Sort, optimizing overall sorting speeds.\n*   **C++ std::sort (Introsort)**: Introsort switches from Quicksort/Heapsort to Insertion Sort for small partitions (typically size $\\le 16$).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Insertion Sort List (LeetCode 147)](https://leetcode.com/problems/insertion-sort-list/): Sort a linked list.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practice adjusting pointer links recursively instead of array shifts.\n* [Sort Colors (LeetCode 75)](https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-colors/): Tri-color sorting.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practice simple in-place element swaps.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Sort an Array (LeetCode 912)](https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-an-array/): Sorting benchmarks.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Highlights when $O(N \\log N)$ sorting algorithms (like Merge Sort) are preferred over Insertion Sort.\n* [Sort List (LeetCode 148)](https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-list/): Logarithmic list sorting.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Compare insertion list sorting with recursive merge list sorting.\n",
