  "slug": "game-math-euler",
  "markdown": "# Game Mathematics: Euler Angles (Yaw, Pitch & Roll)\n\nEuler angles represent a 3D orientation by breaking it down into three consecutive rotations around coordinate axes. They are commonly used in user interfaces because they align with intuitive physical movements.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Defining Yaw, Pitch, and Roll\n\nIn aviation and game development, the three rotations are defined as:\n1.  **Yaw:** Rotation left or right around the local Up axis.\n2.  **Pitch:** Rotation tilting nose up or down around the local Right axis.\n3.  **Roll:** Tilt tilting the wings around the local Forward axis.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 240\" width=\"100%\" height=\"240\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Axis Helper System -->\n  <!-- Y Axis (Up - Yaw) -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-emerald)\" />\n  <text x=\"260\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"13\">Y (Yaw / Up)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Yaw circular rotation arc -->\n  <path d=\"M 230 110 A 25 15 0 1 1 270 110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\" />\n  <path d=\"M 270 110 L 273 103 L 265 107 z\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n\n  <!-- X Axis (Right - Pitch) -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  <text x=\"390\" y=\"154\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"13\">X (Pitch / Right)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Pitch circular rotation arc -->\n  <path d=\"M 310 135 A 15 25 0 1 1 310 165\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\" />\n  <path d=\"M 310 165 L 303 168 L 307 160 z\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n\n  <!-- Z Axis (Forward - Roll) -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"225\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"13\">Z (Roll / Forward)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Roll circular rotation arc -->\n  <path d=\"M 185 180 A 20 20 0 1 1 205 195\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\" />\n  <path d=\"M 205 195 L 208 187 L 200 191 z\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nCoordinate axis mappings differ by engine:\n*   **Unity (Y-Up system):** Yaw is around the Y-axis, Pitch is around the X-axis, Roll is around the Z-axis.\n*   **Unreal Engine (Z-Up system):** Yaw is around the Z-axis, Pitch is around the Y-axis, Roll is around the X-axis.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Rotation Order Dependency\n\nBecause 3D rotations are non-commutative (applying rotation A then B does not yield the same result as B then A), the order in which Euler rotations are applied is critical. Changing the rotation order changes the resulting orientation.\n\nStandard rotation orders in engines:\n*   **Unity:** ZXY (Roll first, then Pitch, then Yaw).\n*   **Unreal Engine:** ZYX (Yaw first, then Pitch, then Roll).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Euler to Matrix Conversion\n\nTo represent Euler angles $(\\theta_x, \\theta_y, \\theta_z)$ as a single rotation matrix, we multiply the individual axis rotation matrices in our chosen order.\n\nFor the **YXZ** rotation order (Pitch first, then Yaw, then Roll):\n\n$$R_{\\text{euler}} = R_y(\\theta_y) \\times R_x(\\theta_x) \\times R_z(\\theta_z)$$\n\nExpanding this multiplication yields a matrix where every element is a trigonometric combination of the sines and cosines of the three angles.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Gimbal Lock\n\nGimbal lock is a mathematical collapse that occurs when two of the three rotation axes align. \n\nFor example, in a system with YXZ rotation order, if the Pitch ($\\theta_x$) rotation reaches exactly $90^\\circ$ ($\\sin(90^\\circ) = 1, \\cos(90^\\circ) = 0$), the Yaw and Roll axes align. \n\n```\nOuter Ring (Yaw) ────┐\nInner Ring (Roll) ───┼──> (Aligning two axes collapses \nMiddle Ring (Pitch) ─┘     the system into 2D rotation!)\n```\n\nMathematically, the expanded rotation matrix collapses to:\n\n$$R_{\\text{euler}} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos(\\theta_y + \\theta_z) & 0 & \\sin(\\theta_y + \\theta_z) \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\sin(\\theta_y + \\theta_z) & 0 & \\cos(\\theta_y + \\theta_z) \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nBecause Yaw ($\\theta_y$) and Roll ($\\theta_z$) are now added together inside the trigonometric operations, they modify the same rotational plane. The system loses a degree of freedom, preventing any rotation perpendicular to that plane until the Pitch angle is changed.\n\nTo avoid this limitation, game engines use unit quaternions for internal rotation calculations, using Euler angles only for user input and interface representation.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef euler_to_matrix_yxz(pitch_deg, yaw_deg, roll_deg):\n    p = math.radians(pitch_deg)\n    y = math.radians(yaw_deg)\n    r = math.radians(roll_deg)\n    \n    sp, cp = math.sin(p), math.cos(p)\n    sy, cy = math.sin(y), math.cos(y)\n    sr, cr = math.sin(r), math.cos(r)\n    \n    # Pre-multiplied YXZ rotation matrix\n    m = [\n        [cy*cr + sy*sp*sr, -cy*sr + sy*sp*cr, sy*cp],\n        [cp*sr,            cp*cr,            -sp],\n        [-sy*cr + cy*sp*sr, sy*sr + cy*sp*cr, cy*cp]\n    ]\n    return m\n```\n",
  "title": "Euler Angle",
