  "slug": "game-math-catmull",
  "markdown": "# Game Mathematics: Catmull-Rom Splines\n\nA **Catmull-Rom spline** is a cubic interpolating spline. Unlike Bezier curves, where the curve is pulled toward the control points but does not touch them, a Catmull-Rom spline **passes exactly through its control points** (except for the first and last). \n\nThis makes it the preferred tool in game engines for creating smooth tracks, camera paths, or paths for AI characters, as you can guarantee that the object will pass directly through specific checkpoints.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Mathematical Formulation\n\nA single curve segment interpolates between point $\\mathbf{P}_1$ and $\\mathbf{P}_2$. It uses the preceding point $\\mathbf{P}_0$ and the succeeding point $\\mathbf{P}_3$ to calculate the tangents (directions) at $\\mathbf{P}_1$ and $\\mathbf{P}_2$.\n\nThe tangent $\\mathbf{T}_i$ at point $\\mathbf{P}_i$ is calculated as half the direction vector from the previous point to the next:\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}_i = \\frac{\\mathbf{P}_{i+1} - \\mathbf{P}_{i-1}}{2}$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Knot Points -->\n  <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"160\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" />\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"185\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">P0</text>\n  \n  <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"80\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"175\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">P1</text>\n  \n  <circle cx=\"320\" cy=\"140\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"315\" y=\"165\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">P2</text>\n  \n  <circle cx=\"440\" cy=\"60\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" />\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"85\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">P3</text>\n\n  <!-- Curve Segment between P1 and P2 -->\n  <path d=\"M 180 80 C 240 50 260 170 320 140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"115\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">Interpolated Path</text>\n  \n  <!-- Tangent guides -->\n  <!-- Tangent at P1 (P2 - P0)/2 -->\n  <line x1=\"180\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"245\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n  <!-- Tangent at P2 (P3 - P1)/2 -->\n  <line x1=\"320\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Catmull-Rom Matrix\n\nBy representing the spline segment between $\\mathbf{P}_1$ and $\\mathbf{P}_2$ in matrix form:\n\n$$\\mathbf{P}(t) = 0.5 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix} t^3 & t^2 & t & 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} \\mathbf{P}_0 \\\\ \\mathbf{P}_1 \\\\ \\mathbf{P}_2 \\\\ \\mathbf{P}_3 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad t \\in [0, 1]$$\n\nThis matrix calculates the curve segment efficiently using standard matrix multiplication and basis vector operations.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Parameterization Types\n\nBased on how the parameter interval is mapped to control points, there are three types of Catmull-Rom splines:\n*   **Uniform Spline ($\\alpha = 0$):** Simple, fast, but suffers from loops and self-intersections when control points are spaced unevenly.\n*   **Centripetal Spline ($\\alpha = 0.5$):** Prevents self-intersections, cusps, and overshoot. It is the **industry standard** for camera paths and track layout.\n*   **Chordal Spline ($\\alpha = 1.0$):** Stretches the curve out, yielding a looser fit.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass CatmullRomSpline:\n    def __init__(self, p0, p1, p2, p3):\n        # Control points coordinates as tuples (x, y)\n        self.p0 = p0\n        self.p1 = p1\n        self.p2 = p2\n        self.p3 = p3\n        \n    def evaluate(self, t):\n        t2 = t * t\n        t3 = t2 * t\n        \n        # Matrix weights calculation\n        w0 = -t3 + 2*t2 - t\n        w1 = 3*t3 - 5*t2 + 2\n        w2 = -3*t3 + 4*t2 + t\n        w3 = t3 - t2\n        \n        # Blend points component-wise\n        x = 0.5 * (w0*self.p0[0] + w1*self.p1[0] + w2*self.p2[0] + w3*self.p3[0])\n        y = 0.5 * (w0*self.p0[1] + w1*self.p1[1] + w2*self.p2[1] + w3*self.p3[1])\n        \n        return (x, y)\n```\n",
  "title": "Catmull-Rom Spline",
  "tagline": "Interpolating spline formulation with automatic tangent calculation.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
