  "slug": "game-engine-native",
  "markdown": "# Game Engines: Native Architecture (From Scratch)\n\nBuilding a game engine from scratch (a native engine) involves interfacing directly with operating system APIs and hardware drivers. Unlike using pre-built engines, writing a native engine requires you to implement the **Game Loop**, **Window Management**, **Input Polling**, and **Rendering Pipelines** manually.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Core Game Loop\n\nThe heartbeat of any game engine is the **Game Loop**. It runs continuously, updating game logic and rendering frames to the screen. \n\nA naive game loop updates as fast as the CPU allows, but this causes the game speed to vary wildly on different computers. To prevent this, we write a **Fixed Time-step Game Loop with Accumulator**:\n\n```\n        ┌─────────────────────────┐\n        │   Poll OS Window Events │\n        └────────────┬────────────┘\n                     │\n        ┌────────────▼────────────┐\n        │ Calculate Delta Time    │\n        └────────────┬────────────┘\n                     │\n        ┌────────────▼────────────┐  Loop while\n        │ Update Physics          │ <── accumulator >= fixed_step\n        │ (Fixed Timestep)        │\n        └────────────┬────────────┘\n                     │\n        ┌────────────▼────────────┐\n        │ Render Frame            │\n        └────────────┬────────────┘\n                     │\n        ┌────────────▼────────────┐\n        │ Present to Screen       │\n        └────────────┬────────────┘\n                     │\n                     └─ (Repeat Loop)\n```\n\n### The Accumulator Algorithm\nWe keep physics and collision updates independent of the rendering frame rate. We accumulate the time elapsed in each frame and run the update step in fixed slices (e.g. $0.016$s for 60 updates/sec):\n\n```cpp\ndouble accumulator = 0.0;\ndouble fixed_timestep = 0.01666; // 60 Hz\n\nwhile (running) {\n    double frame_time = get_elapsed_time();\n    accumulator += frame_time;\n    \n    // Process input events\n    poll_events();\n    \n    // Update physics in fixed increments\n    while (accumulator >= fixed_timestep) {\n        update_physics(fixed_timestep);\n        accumulator -= fixed_timestep;\n    }\n    \n    // Render as fast as possible, interpolating remaining time\n    double alpha = accumulator / fixed_timestep;\n    render_game(alpha);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Platform Windowing and Inputs\n\nA native engine must initialize an OS window and receive hardware keyboard/mouse/gamepad events. Instead of writing raw platform code for every OS (Win32 for Windows, Cocoa for macOS, X11/Wayland for Linux), developers use cross-platform helper libraries:\n\n*   **GLFW:** A lightweight library focused on window creation and OpenGL/Vulkan/Metal context setup.\n*   **SDL (Simple DirectMedia Layer):** A comprehensive framework that handles windowing, inputs, audio mixing, thread creation, and basic 2D graphics.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Rendering Pipeline\n\nTo draw pixels on the screen, a native engine communicates with the graphics card using APIs like **Vulkan**, **DirectX**, or **OpenGL**.\n\n### 3.1 Swapchains\nYou cannot write pixel data directly to the monitor's display memory, as it would cause flickering (screen tearing). Instead, engines use a **Swapchain** containing multiple frame buffers:\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 180\" width=\"100%\" height=\"180\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Back Buffer (GPU Rendering) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(40, 40)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"150\" height=\"90\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"4\" />\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">Back Buffer</text>\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">GPU is rendering</text>\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">next frame here...</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- Swap Action Arrow -->\n  <path d=\"M 205 85 L 285 85\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-emerald)\" />\n  <text x=\"215\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">SWAP</text>\n  \n  <!-- Front Buffer (Monitor Display) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(310, 40)\">\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"150\" height=\"90\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"4\" />\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">Front Buffer</text>\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Monitor is displaying</text>\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">this frame now.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n1.  **Back Buffer:** The GPU renders the upcoming frame to this hidden buffer.\n2.  **Front Buffer:** The monitor displays the finished frame from this buffer.\n3.  **Swap:** Once rendering is complete, the buffers swap. The back buffer becomes the front buffer, and vice versa.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Systems Architecture\n\nA native engine coordinates several core loops:\n\n*   **Render Thread:** Streams vertex data to buffers, binds textures, compiles shader pipelines, and submits command queues to the GPU.\n*   **Physics Engine:** Solves rigid body dynamics, detects colliders, and resolves intersections using spatial partitioning trees.\n*   **Audio Engine:** Decodes audio files, spatializes sound sources based on 3D distance vectors, and writes output buffers to the system audio card.\n",
  "title": "Native Engine Architecture",
  "tagline": "Building custom engines from scratch with swapchains, windowing, and fixed physics loops.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
