  "slug": "game-phys-mass-center",
  "markdown": "# Game Physics: Center of Mass\n\nEvery rigid body possesses a unique point at which the entire mass of the body can be assumed to be concentrated for the purposes of calculating translation and rotation. This point is the **Center of Mass (CoM)**. \n\nWhen a force is applied directly through the center of mass, the object translates (moves linearly) without rotating. If a force is applied offset from the center of mass, it generates torque, causing the object to both translate and rotate.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Discrete Center of Mass Calculation\n\nFor a system consisting of $N$ discrete particles, where each particle $i$ has mass $m_i$ and a position vector $\\mathbf{r}_i = (x_i, y_i, z_i)$, the center of mass $\\mathbf{R}_{\\text{CoM}}$ is the weighted average of their positions:\n\n$$\\mathbf{R}_{\\text{CoM}} = \\frac{1}{M} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} m_i \\mathbf{r}_i$$\n\nWhere $M$ is the total mass of the system:\n$$M = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} m_i$$\n\n---\n\n## 2. Composite Bodies\n\nIn game engines, complex objects are often constructed by combining simpler primitive shapes (like boxes, spheres, and cylinders). If we know the mass $M_j$ and center of mass $\\mathbf{C}_j$ of each individual primitive $j$, we can calculate the composite center of mass $\\mathbf{R}_{\\text{composite}}$:\n\n$$\\mathbf{R}_{\\text{composite}} = \\frac{\\sum M_j \\mathbf{C}_j}{\\sum M_j}$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Grid Lines -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Primitive 1 (Large Box) -->\n  <rect x=\"100\" y=\"80\" width=\"120\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"4\" />\n  <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"120\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <text x=\"135\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">C1 (m1=4kg)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Primitive 2 (Small Box) -->\n  <rect x=\"220\" y=\"110\" width=\"80\" height=\"50\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"4\" />\n  <circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"135\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">C2 (m2=1kg)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Composite Center of Mass (Shifted towards C1) -->\n  <!-- R = (4*160 + 1*260) / 5 = 180. y = (4*120 + 1*135)/5 = 123 -->\n  <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"123\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <line x1=\"180\" y1=\"123\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Composite CoM (180, 123)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Continuous Center of Mass Calculation\n\nFor a continuous solid body with density function $\\rho(\\mathbf{r})$, the summation becomes an integration over the volume $V$ of the object:\n\n$$\\mathbf{R}_{\\text{CoM}} = \\frac{1}{M} \\int_{V} \\rho(\\mathbf{r}) \\mathbf{r} \\, dV$$\n\nWhere the total mass is:\n$$M = \\int_{V} \\rho(\\mathbf{r}) \\, dV$$\n\nIf the body has uniform density (homogenous), the center of mass is equal to the geometric centroid of the shape.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Dynamic Center of Mass Shifts\n\nIn games, the center of mass of an entity can change dynamically during gameplay:\n*   A vehicle firing shells, changing its weight distribution.\n*   A character picking up a heavy weapon on their right side, shifting their balance center.\n\nWhen attachments are added or removed, the physics engine updates the center of mass to ensure correct rotational dynamics:\n\n$$\\mathbf{R}_{\\text{new}} = \\frac{M_{\\text{old}} \\mathbf{R}_{\\text{old}} + m_{\\text{attachment}} \\mathbf{r}_{\\text{attachment}}}{M_{\\text{old}} + m_{\\text{attachment}}}$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 180\" width=\"100%\" height=\"180\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Car frame outline -->\n  <path d=\"M 100 130 L 120 90 L 380 90 L 400 130 z\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"140\" r=\"20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"140\" r=\"20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <!-- Original CoM (Center) -->\n  <circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"110\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Original CoM</text>\n  \n  <!-- Added Mass (Rear Cargo) -->\n  <rect x=\"80\" y=\"80\" width=\"40\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <text x=\"75\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Added Cargo</text>\n  \n  <!-- Shifted CoM (Rear-wards) -->\n  <path d=\"M 250 110 L 210 115\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  <circle cx=\"210\" cy=\"115\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"135\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Shifted CoM</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass PhysicsObject:\n    def __init__(self, base_mass, base_com):\n        self.mass = float(base_mass)\n        self.com = list(base_com) # [x, y, z]\n        \n    def add_attachment(self, attachment_mass, attachment_pos):\n        m_old = self.mass\n        m_att = float(attachment_mass)\n        \n        # Calculate new total mass\n        m_new = m_old + m_att\n        \n        # Weighted average shift\n        new_x = (m_old * self.com[0] + m_att * attachment_pos[0]) / m_new\n        new_y = (m_old * self.com[1] + m_att * attachment_pos[1]) / m_new\n        new_z = (m_old * self.com[2] + m_att * attachment_pos[2]) / m_new\n        \n        self.mass = m_new\n        self.com = [new_x, new_y, new_z]\n\n# Test: Object at origin (0,0,0) of mass 10kg gets a 5kg attachment at (3, 0, 0)\nobj = PhysicsObject(10.0, (0.0, 0.0, 0.0))\nobj.add_attachment(5.0, (3.0, 0.0, 0.0))\nprint(\"New CoM position:\", obj.com) # Expected: (1.0, 0.0, 0.0)\n```\n",
  "title": "Center of Mass",
  "tagline": "Discrete and continuous center of mass calculations and composite shapes.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
