  "slug": "game-phys-inertia",
  "markdown": "# Game Physics: Moment of Inertia\n\nWhile mass measures an object's resistance to **linear acceleration**, the **Moment of Inertia ($I$)** measures an object's resistance to **angular acceleration** (rotation). \n\nUnlike linear mass, which is a single scalar number, the moment of inertia depends on the object's shape and how its mass is distributed relative to the axis of rotation.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Single Particle and Continuous Shapes\n\nFor a single point mass $m$ rotating at a distance $r$ from an axis:\n\n$$I = m r^2$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 180\" width=\"100%\" height=\"180\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Axis of rotation (Center point) -->\n  <circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"90\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"12\">Axis of Rotation</text>\n  \n  <!-- Mass point -->\n  <circle cx=\"380\" cy=\"90\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <text x=\"375\" y=\"94\" fill=\"#141416\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">m</text>\n  \n  <!-- Radius line -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"368\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"305\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"13\">r</text>\n  \n  <!-- Rotation circular path -->\n  <path d=\"M 250 30 A 60 60 0 0 1 310 90\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">I = m * r²</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nFor a continuous solid body, the moment of inertia is the integral of all mass elements $dm$ multiplied by their squared distance $r^2$ to the axis:\n\n$$I = \\int r^2 \\, dm = \\int \\rho(\\mathbf{r}) r^2 \\, dV$$\n\n---\n\n## 2. Standard Moments of Inertia (Centroidal Axes)\n\nIn game physics engines, the moments of inertia for standard geometric shapes are calculated using these closed-form solutions:\n\n*   **Solid Sphere (Radius $r$, Mass $m$):**\n    $$I = \\frac{2}{5} m r^2$$\n*   **Hollow Thin Sphere (Radius $r$, Mass $m$):**\n    $$I = \\frac{2}{3} m r^2$$\n*   **Solid Cuboid (Width $w$, Height $h$, Depth $d$, Mass $m$):**\n    $$I_{xx} = \\frac{1}{12} m (h^2 + d^2), \\quad I_{yy} = \\frac{1}{12} m (w^2 + d^2), \\quad I_{zz} = \\frac{1}{12} m (w^2 + h^2)$$\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Inertia Tensor\n\nIn 3D space, an object can rotate around any arbitrary axis. To describe rotation in 3D, the moment of inertia is represented by a symmetric $3 \\times 3$ matrix called the **Inertia Tensor ($I$)**:\n\n$$I = \\begin{bmatrix} I_{xx} & I_{xy} & I_{xz} \\\\ I_{yx} & I_{yy} & I_{yz} \\\\ I_{zx} & I_{zy} & I_{zz} \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n*   **Diagonal Terms ($I_{xx}, I_{yy}, I_{zz}$):** The moments of inertia around the principal local X, Y, and Z axes.\n*   **Off-Diagonal Terms ($I_{xy}, I_{xz}, \\dots$):** The *products of inertia*. If the coordinate axes align perfectly with the object's principal axes of symmetry, the off-diagonal terms become $0$, simplifying the matrix to a diagonal matrix.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The Parallel Axis Theorem\n\nIf you know the moment of inertia $I_{\\text{cm}}$ of a body rotating around its center of mass, you can find the moment of inertia $I$ around a parallel axis that is offset by a distance $d$ using the **Parallel Axis Theorem**:\n\n$$I = I_{\\text{cm}} + m d^2$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Arbitrary Shape -->\n  <path d=\"M 200 60 Q 150 120 220 160 Q 300 170 330 110 Q 340 50 250 40 Z\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <!-- Center of mass axis -->\n  <circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">CoM (I_cm)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Offset axis -->\n  <circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <text x=\"330\" y=\"85\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Offset Axis (I)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Distance line -->\n  <line x1=\"240\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  <text x=\"285\" y=\"115\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"13\">d</text>\n  \n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"185\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"13\">I = I_cm + m * d²</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nA classic application is a swinging pendulum or physical door hinge. Instead of rotating around its center of mass, the door rotates around its edge hinge. We use this theorem to calculate the correct rotational drag and inertia relative to the hinge axis.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Example (Python)\n\nHere is a Python function calculating the diagonal inertia tensor of a solid cuboid:\n\n```python\nclass CuboidRigidBody:\n    def __init__(self, mass, width, height, depth):\n        self.mass = float(mass)\n        self.w = float(width)\n        self.h = float(height)\n        self.d = float(depth)\n        \n    def get_principal_inertia_tensor(self):\n        # Calculate diagonal tensor components\n        # Ixx = 1/12 * m * (h^2 + d^2)\n        # Iyy = 1/12 * m * (w^2 + d^2)\n        # Izz = 1/12 * m * (w^2 + h^2)\n        m_factor = self.mass / 12.0\n        \n        ixx = m_factor * (self.h**2 + self.d**2)\n        iyy = m_factor * (self.w**2 + self.d**2)\n        izz = m_factor * (self.w**2 + self.h**2)\n        \n        return [\n            [ixx, 0.0, 0.0],\n            [0.0, iyy, 0.0],\n            [0.0, 0.0, izz]\n        ]\n```\n",
  "title": "Moment of Inertia",
  "tagline": "Resistance to rotational acceleration, inertia tensors, and parallel axis theorem.",
