  "slug": "game-phys-joints",
  "markdown": "# Game Physics: Joint Constraints\n\nIn game physics, rigid bodies are connected to form complex mechanisms (like ragdolls, doors, or vehicle suspensions) using **Joint Constraints**. A constraint limits the relative movement (degrees of freedom) between two bodies.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Constraint Mathematics\n\nA constraint is defined by a mathematical function of the position $\\mathbf{x}$ and orientation $\\mathbf{q}$ of two bodies.\n\n### 1.1 Position Constraint\nA position constraint equation $C$ equals $0$ when the constraint is satisfied:\n\n$$C(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\mathbf{q}_2) = 0$$\n\n### 1.2 Velocity Constraint\nTo solve constraints inside a velocity-based solver, we take the time derivative of the position constraint, yielding the velocity constraint equation:\n\n$$\\dot{C} = J \\mathbf{v} = 0$$\n\nWhere:\n*   **$J$** is the **Jacobian Matrix** (which defines the direction of the constraint force).\n*   **$\\mathbf{v}$** is the combined velocity vector of both bodies: $\\mathbf{v} = [\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{\\omega}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{\\omega}_2]^T$.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Body 1 -->\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"60\" width=\"100\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"4\" />\n  <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"95\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Body 1</text>\n  \n  <!-- Body 2 -->\n  <rect x=\"330\" y=\"70\" width=\"110\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"4\" />\n  <circle cx=\"385\" cy=\"105\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <text x=\"375\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Body 2</text>\n  \n  <!-- Joint Connection Link -->\n  <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"385\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"120\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"145\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">Joint Pivot Point</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Common Joint Types\n\nRigid bodies in 3D have 6 degrees of freedom (3 translation, 3 rotation). Joints lock specific degrees of freedom:\n\n*   **Distance Joint:** Locks 1 translational degree of freedom. It keeps two points at a fixed distance $d$:\n    $$C = \\|\\mathbf{p}_2 - \\mathbf{p}_1\\| - d = 0$$\n*   **Ball-and-Socket Joint:** Locks 3 translational degrees of freedom. It aligns a point on Body 1 with a point on Body 2, allowing free rotation in all directions:\n    $$C = \\mathbf{p}_2 - \\mathbf{p}_1 = \\mathbf{0}$$\n*   **Hinge / Revolute Joint:** Locks 3 translational and 2 rotational degrees of freedom, allowing rotation around only a single local axis.\n*   **Slider Joint:** Locks 2 translational and 3 rotational degrees of freedom, allowing translation along only a single axis.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Resolving Violations: Sequential Impulses\n\nIf numerical integration causes joint positions to drift (violating the constraint), the physics engine applies correction impulses $\\mathbf{P}_c$.\n\nWe calculate the Lagrange multiplier (impulse scalar $\\lambda$) needed to resolve the velocity error:\n\n$$\\lambda = -\\frac{J \\mathbf{v} + \\beta \\frac{C}{\\Delta t}}{J M^{-1} J^T}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   **$M^{-1}$** is the inverse mass/inertia matrix of the bodies.\n*   **$\\beta$** is the **Baumgarte stabilization factor** (typically $0.1$ to $0.2$) which determines how fast position drift errors ($C$) are corrected.\n\nThe correction impulse vector is then applied to the bodies:\n\n$$\\mathbf{P}_c = J^T \\lambda$$\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python)\n\nHere is a Python class evaluating the error of a 2D Distance Joint:\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nclass DistanceJoint:\n    def __init__(self, body_a, body_b, local_anchor_a, local_anchor_b, target_distance):\n        self.body_a = body_a\n        self.body_b = body_b\n        self.anchor_a = local_anchor_a # Offset from body A center\n        self.anchor_b = local_anchor_b # Offset from body B center\n        self.target_dist = float(target_distance)\n        \n    def calculate_constraint_error(self):\n        # 1. Calculate global anchor positions\n        global_a_x = self.body_a.pos[0] + self.anchor_a[0]\n        global_a_y = self.body_a.pos[1] + self.anchor_a[1]\n        \n        global_b_x = self.body_b.pos[0] + self.anchor_b[0]\n        global_b_y = self.body_b.pos[1] + self.anchor_b[1]\n        \n        # 2. Distance vector\n        dx = global_b_x - global_a_x\n        dy = global_b_y - global_a_y\n        current_dist = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n        \n        # 3. Position error (Constraint value C)\n        error = current_dist - self.target_dist\n        \n        # Normal direction vector of constraint\n        nx = dx / current_dist if current_dist > 0.0 else 1.0\n        ny = dy / current_dist if current_dist > 0.0 else 0.0\n        \n        return error, (nx, ny)\n```\n",
  "title": "Joint Constraints",
  "tagline": "Degrees of freedom, Jacobians, and Sequential Impulse solvers.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
  "readTime": 15,
  "lastUpdated": "July 2026",
