  "slug": "game-phys-force",
  "markdown": "# Game Physics: Force & Acceleration\n\nIn physics engines, forces are the inputs that change a rigid body's state of motion. A **Force ($\\mathbf{F}$)** changes an object's linear momentum over time. By summing all forces acting on an object, we determine its linear acceleration.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Newtonian Force Accumulation\n\nAccording to Newton's Second Law of Motion:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F}_{\\text{net}} = \\sum \\mathbf{F}_i = m \\mathbf{a}$$\n\nIn a physics engine's update loop, forces from different sources (such as gravity, wind, user input, and collisions) are accumulated into a single net force vector during the frame:\n\n```\nFrame Start ──> Clear Forces (F_net = 0)\n                 │\n                 ├──> Apply Gravity (F_net += m * g)\n                 ├──> Apply User Thrust (F_net += F_thrust)\n                 ├──> Apply Wind Resistance (F_net += F_drag)\n                 │\nUpdate Loop  ──> Solve Acceleration (a = F_net / m)\n                 │\n                 └──> Integrate Velocity & Reset Force Buffer\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. In-Engine Forces\n\n### 2.1 Gravity\nGravity is a constant acceleration field applied to all bodies regardless of mass:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F}_g = m \\mathbf{g}$$\n\n### 2.2 Aerodynamic Drag (Air Resistance)\nAs an object moves through a fluid (like air or water), it experiences drag. The drag force operates opposite to the velocity vector and increases quadratically with speed:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F}_d = -\\frac{1}{2} \\rho C_d A \\|\\mathbf{v}\\| \\mathbf{v}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   **$\\rho$** is the density of the fluid.\n*   **$C_d$** is the drag coefficient (determined by shape).\n*   **$A$** is the frontal cross-sectional area.\n*   **$\\mathbf{v}$** is the velocity vector.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Free-Body Diagrams (Inclined Planes)\n\nA classic physics engine test is a block sliding down an inclined plane. The block experiences three competing force vectors:\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Incline Plane Triangle -->\n  <path d=\"M 80 180 L 420 180 L 420 60 Z\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Sliding Block -->\n  <!-- Center is approx (250, 125). Incline angle is approx 20 degrees -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(250,125) rotate(-19.5)\">\n    <rect x=\"-30\" y=\"-20\" width=\"60\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"2\" />\n    <circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n    \n    <!-- Normal Force (Perpendicular to slope - points UP) -->\n    <line x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"-70\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-emerald)\" />\n    <text x=\"5\" y=\"-55\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">FN (Normal)</text>\n    \n    <!-- Friction Force (Parallel to slope - points UP-LEFT) -->\n    <line x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"-60\" y2=\"0\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n    <text x=\"-70\" y=\"-10\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Ff (Friction)</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- Gravity Force (Always points directly DOWN - unrotated) -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  <text x=\"260\" y=\"195\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fg (Gravity)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n*   **Gravity ($\\mathbf{F}_g$):** Pulls directly down toward the center of the earth.\n*   **Normal Force ($\\mathbf{F}_N$):** Pushes perpendicular to the surface of the incline, preventing the block from sinking into it.\n*   **Friction ($\\mathbf{F}_f$):** Opposes sliding, acting parallel to the surface in the direction opposite to the movement.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass PhysicsObject:\n    def __init__(self, mass):\n        self.mass = float(mass)\n        self.position = [0.0, 0.0]\n        self.velocity = [0.0, 0.0]\n        self.accumulated_force = [0.0, 0.0]\n        \n    def clear_forces(self):\n        self.accumulated_force = [0.0, 0.0]\n        \n    def apply_force(self, force_vector):\n        self.accumulated_force[0] += force_vector[0]\n        self.accumulated_force[1] += force_vector[1]\n        \n    def update_physics(self, dt, gravity_accel=-9.8):\n        # 1. Apply gravity: Fg = m * g\n        self.apply_force([0.0, self.mass * gravity_accel])\n        \n        # 2. Apply dynamic air resistance (drag)\n        # Fd = -0.5 * drag_coeff * velocity * speed\n        drag_coeff = 0.1\n        speed = math.sqrt(self.velocity[0]**2 + self.velocity[1]**2)\n        if speed > 0.0:\n            fd_x = -0.5 * drag_coeff * self.velocity[0] * speed\n            fd_y = -0.5 * drag_coeff * self.velocity[1] * speed\n            self.apply_force([fd_x, fd_y])\n            \n        # 3. Acceleration: a = F / m\n        accel_x = self.accumulated_force[0] / self.mass\n        accel_y = self.accumulated_force[1] / self.mass\n        \n        # 4. Integrate velocity\n        self.velocity[0] += accel_x * dt\n        self.velocity[1] += accel_y * dt\n        \n        # 5. Integrate position\n        self.position[0] += self.velocity[0] * dt\n        self.position[1] += self.velocity[1] * dt\n        \n        # 6. Reset accumulator\n        self.clear_forces()\n```\n",
  "title": "Force & Momentum",
  "tagline": "Newtonian force accumulation, air resistance/drag models, and gravity dynamics.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
  "readTime": 15,
  "lastUpdated": "July 2026",
