  "slug": "game-phys-buoyancy",
  "markdown": "# Game Physics: Buoyancy & Floating Stability\n\n**Buoyancy** is the upward force exerted by a fluid on an object placed in it. Implementing buoyancy is essential for simulating ships, floating boxes, or players swimming in water.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Archimedes' Principle\n\nAccording to Archimedes' Principle, any object submerged in a fluid experiences an upward force equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces:\n\n$$F_b = \\rho g V_{\\text{submerged}}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   **$\\rho$** is the density of the fluid (e.g. $\\sim 1000 \\text{ kg/m}^3$ for water).\n*   **$g$** is the acceleration due to gravity.\n*   **$V_{\\text{submerged}}$** is the volume of the object that is below the fluid's surface.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Center of Buoyancy vs. Center of Mass\n\nTo apply buoyancy forces correctly in a rigid body solver, we must track two different reference points:\n\n*   **Center of Mass (CoM):** The point where the gravity force vector ($F_g = mg$) acts.\n*   **Center of Buoyancy (CoB):** The geometric centroid of the *submerged volume* of the object. The buoyancy force vector ($F_b$) acts at this point.\n\nIf the object is uniform and fully submerged, the CoB aligns with the CoM. However, if the object is only partially submerged, or has a non-uniform shape, these two points will be offset.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Water Surface Line -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 2\" />\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Water Surface</text>\n  \n  <!-- Submerged Box -->\n  <rect x=\"200\" y=\"60\" width=\"100\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"4\" />\n  \n  <!-- Center of Mass CoM (Physical Center) -->\n  <circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  <text x=\"260\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Fg (Gravity at CoM)</text>\n\n  <!-- Center of Buoyancy CoB (Centroid of bottom half) -->\n  <!-- Submerged portion is from y=100 to y=140. Centroid is at y=120 -->\n  <circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"120\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  <text x=\"260\" y=\"65\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Fb (Buoyancy at CoB)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Rotational Stability & Righting Torque\n\nWhen a floating vessel (like a boat) tilts, the shape of the submerged volume changes. This shifts the Center of Buoyancy (CoB) sideways relative to the Center of Mass (CoM).\n\nThis offset generates a **Righting Torque** that rotates the vessel back to an upright position:\n\n$$\\mathbf{\\tau}_{\\text{buoyant}} = (\\mathbf{C}_{\\text{Buoyancy}} - \\mathbf{C}_{\\text{Mass}}) \\times \\mathbf{F}_b$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Water Surface -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Tilted Hull (Rotated representation) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(250,110) rotate(15)\">\n    <!-- Hull Box -->\n    <path d=\"M -80 -20 L 80 -20 L 60 40 L -60 40 Z\" fill=\"#27272a\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n    \n    <!-- CoM (High up) -->\n    <circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n    <text x=\"10\" y=\"5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">CoM</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- Gravity Vector (directly down from rotated CoM) -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n\n  <!-- Tilted Submerged Centroid (Shifted Rightwards in global coords) -->\n  <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"130\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"145\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Shifted CoB</text>\n  \n  <!-- Buoyancy Vector (directly up from shifted CoB) -->\n  <line x1=\"280\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n  \n  <!-- Righting Torque indicator -->\n  <path d=\"M 230 75 Q 250 60 270 75\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-emerald)\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"50\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Restoring Righting Torque</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nIf the Center of Mass is too high (above the metacentre of the hull), the torque will rotate the vessel further, causing it to capsize.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass FloatingObject:\n    def __init__(self, mass, com, dimensions):\n        self.mass = float(mass)\n        self.com = com # (x, y)\n        self.w, self.h = dimensions\n        self.velocity = [0.0, 0.0]\n        \n    def solve_buoyancy(self, water_y, water_density=1.0, gravity=-9.8):\n        # 1. Calculate submerged depth\n        bottom_y = self.com[1] - self.h / 2.0\n        top_y = self.com[1] + self.h / 2.0\n        \n        if bottom_y >= water_y:\n            # Completely out of water\n            submerged_ratio = 0.0\n        elif top_y <= water_y:\n            # Completely underwater\n            submerged_ratio = 1.0\n        else:\n            # Partially submerged\n            submerged_ratio = (water_y - bottom_y) / self.h\n            \n        # 2. Submerged Volume (approximate as area in 2D)\n        volume_submerged = self.w * self.h * submerged_ratio\n        \n        # 3. Buoyancy Force = fluid_density * gravity * displaced_volume\n        # Force acts UPWARD (opposite to gravity)\n        f_buoyant = -water_density * gravity * volume_submerged\n        \n        # 4. Net forces\n        f_gravity = self.mass * gravity\n        f_net = f_gravity + f_buoyant\n        \n        # 5. Integrate acceleration to velocity\n        accel_y = f_net / self.mass\n        return accel_y\n```\n",
  "title": "Buoyancy & Stability",
  "tagline": "Archimedes' principle, centers of buoyancy, and righting torque stabilization.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
  "readTime": 15,
