  "slug": "game-phys-ccd",
  "markdown": "# Game Physics: Continuous Collision Detection (CCD)\n\nIn standard discrete collision detection, the physics engine checks for overlaps at specific intervals in every frame. While this is fast and simple, it has a major limitation: **Tunneling**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Tunneling Problem\n\nIf an object is traveling at a high velocity, or if a collision barrier is thin (such as a bullet fired at a wall), the object can pass completely through the barrier between two frame updates.\n\n```\nFrame n (Before Wall) ───────> Frame n+1 (After Wall)\n          ●       [ Wall ]       ●\n```\n\nBecause the object does not overlap the wall at either `Frame n` or `Frame n+1`, a discrete collision check fails to detect the collision, allowing the object to pass straight through.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Thin Wall -->\n  <rect x=\"240\" y=\"30\" width=\"20\" height=\"140\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"185\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Thin Barrier</text>\n  \n  <!-- Bullet Frame n -->\n  <circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"100\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"100\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#141416\" />\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Frame n (Position)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Path arrow showing jump over wall -->\n  <path d=\"M 95 100 L 390 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  \n  <!-- Bullet Frame n+1 -->\n  <circle cx=\"410\" cy=\"100\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <circle cx=\"410\" cy=\"100\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#141416\" />\n  <text x=\"365\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Frame n+1 (Position)</text>\n  \n  <text x=\"135\" y=\"50\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"13\">Tunneling: No overlap detected!</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Swept Volume Formulation\n\nTo solve tunneling, we use **Continuous Collision Detection (CCD)**. Instead of checking for static overlaps, CCD models the volume swept by the object along its velocity vector over the time interval $t \\in [0, 1]$.\n\nFor example, a **Swept Sphere** traces out a capsule shape:\n\n$$\\text{Capsule}(t) = \\{ \\mathbf{p} + \\tau \\mathbf{v} \\mid \\tau \\in [0, \\Delta t] \\}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   $\\mathbf{p}$ is the sphere's start position.\n*   $\\mathbf{v}$ is the velocity vector.\n*   $\\Delta t$ is the frame time.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Wall -->\n  <rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"30\" height=\"140\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Swept Capsule Area -->\n  <path d=\"M 100 80 L 320 80 A 20 20 0 0 1 320 120 L 100 120 A 20 20 0 0 1 100 80 Z\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.08\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Swept volume capsule</text>\n  \n  <!-- Sphere Start -->\n  <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" r=\"20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <text x=\"75\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">t = 0</text>\n  \n  <!-- Sphere Collision Point (Time of Impact) -->\n  <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"100\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"100\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"280\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Collision at t = TOI</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nWe then perform a raycast using the velocity vector against the target geometries, expanded by the moving object's radius.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Calculating the Time of Impact (TOI)\n\nOnce a collision is detected in the swept volume, the engine must find the exact fraction of the frame time $t_{\\text{TOI}} \\in [0, 1]$ where contact occurs.\n\nFor a moving sphere of radius $r$ hitting a stationary plane defined by a point $\\mathbf{p}_0$ and normal $\\mathbf{n}$, the distance from the sphere to the plane at time $t$ is:\n\n$$d(t) = (\\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v} - \\mathbf{p}_0) \\cdot \\mathbf{n}$$\n\nAt the moment of impact, the distance is exactly equal to the radius:\n$$d(t_{\\text{TOI}}) = r$$\n\nSolving for $t_{\\text{TOI}}$:\n\n$$t_{\\text{TOI}} = \\frac{r - (\\mathbf{p} - \\mathbf{p}_0) \\cdot \\mathbf{n}}{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}}$$\n\nIf $t_{\\text{TOI}} \\in [0, 1]$, a collision occurs. The physics engine then rolls back the object's position to $t_{\\text{TOI}}$, resolves the collision velocity, and uses the remaining time $1 - t_{\\text{TOI}}$ to update the object's trajectory (known as **sub-stepping**).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python)\n\n```python\nclass SweptSphere2D:\n    def __init__(self, start_pos, radius, velocity):\n        self.pos = list(start_pos) # (x, y)\n        self.r = float(radius)\n        self.v = list(velocity)    # (vx, vy)\n        \n    def check_plane_collision(self, plane_origin, plane_normal):\n        # Ray equation: P(t) = P0 + t * V\n        # Plane equation: (P(t) - plane_origin) . normal = r\n        px, py = self.pos\n        vx, vy = self.v\n        ox, oy = plane_origin\n        nx, ny = plane_normal\n        \n        # Calculate dot products\n        v_dot_n = vx * nx + vy * ny\n        \n        # If moving parallel to or away from the plane, no collision\n        if v_dot_n >= 0.0:\n            return None\n            \n        p_minus_o_dot_n = (px - ox) * nx + (py - oy) * ny\n        \n        # Solve for t: t_toi = (r - p_minus_o_dot_n) / v_dot_n\n        t_toi = (self.r - p_minus_o_dot_n) / v_dot_n\n        \n        # Verify if impact occurs within this frame's time step [0.0, 1.0]\n        if 0.0 <= t_toi <= 1.0:\n            return t_toi\n        return None\n```\n",
  "title": "Continuous Collision Detection (CCD)",
  "tagline": "The tunneling problem, swept volume checking, and Time of Impact calculation.",
