  "slug": "game-phys-intersection",
  "markdown": "# Game Physics: Ray Intersection Mathematics\n\nRay casting is a fundamental tool in games used for shooting weapons, camera line-of-sight checks, UI pointer interactions, and pathfinding queries. \n\n---\n\n## 1. Ray Representation\n\nA ray is a parametric line in 3D space starting at an origin point $\\mathbf{o}$ and extending infinitely along a normalized direction vector $\\mathbf{d}$:\n\n$$\\mathbf{R}(t) = \\mathbf{o} + t \\mathbf{d}, \\quad t \\ge 0$$\n\nWhere $t$ represents the distance from the origin along the ray.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Ray-Plane Intersection\n\nA plane is defined by a point $\\mathbf{p}_0$ on the plane and a unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ perpendicular to the surface:\n\n$$(\\mathbf{p} - \\mathbf{p}_0) \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0$$\n\nTo find where the ray intersects the plane, we substitute the ray equation $\\mathbf{R}(t)$ for $\\mathbf{p}$:\n\n$$(\\mathbf{o} + t \\mathbf{d} - \\mathbf{p}_0) \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0$$\n\nSolving for the distance parameter $t$:\n\n$$t = \\frac{(\\mathbf{p}_0 - \\mathbf{o}) \\cdot \\mathbf{n}}{\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}}$$\n\n### Key Cases:\n*   **$\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0$:** The ray is parallel to the plane. If $(\\mathbf{p}_0 - \\mathbf{o}) \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0$, the ray lies inside the plane; otherwise, there is no intersection.\n*   **$t < 0$:** The intersection point lies behind the ray origin, meaning there is no forward intersection.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Ray-Sphere Intersection\n\nA sphere is defined by its center point $\\mathbf{c}$ and radius $r$:\n\n$$\\|\\mathbf{p} - \\mathbf{c}\\|^2 = r^2$$\n\nSubstituting the ray equation $\\mathbf{R}(t)$ for $\\mathbf{p}$:\n\n$$\\|\\mathbf{o} + t \\mathbf{d} - \\mathbf{c}\\|^2 = r^2$$\n\nLet $\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{o} - \\mathbf{c}$. Expanding the vector dot product yields a quadratic equation in terms of $t$:\n\n$$(\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{d}) t^2 + 2(\\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{d}) t + (\\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{w} - r^2) = 0$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{d}$ is normalized ($\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 1$), this simplifies to $a t^2 + b t + c = 0$ with:\n*   $a = 1$\n*   $b = 2(\\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{d})$\n*   $c = \\|\\mathbf{w}\\|^2 - r^2$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 180\" width=\"100%\" height=\"180\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Sphere -->\n  <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"90\" r=\"50\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.08\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"90\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n  <text x=\"275\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Center (c)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Ray Origin -->\n  <circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"130\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Origin (o)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Ray Path -->\n  <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"62\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  \n  <!-- Entry/Exit intersection points -->\n  <circle cx=\"236\" cy=\"99\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">t_entry</text>\n  \n  <circle cx=\"327\" cy=\"81\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"315\" y=\"65\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">t_exit</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nWe solve using the quadratic discriminant:\n$$\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac$$\n\n*   **$\\Delta < 0$:** No intersection (the ray misses the sphere).\n*   **$\\Delta \\ge 0$:** The ray intersects the sphere. The intersection distance is the smaller positive root:\n    $$t = \\frac{-b - \\sqrt{\\Delta}}{2}$$\n\n---\n\n## 4. Ray-AABB Intersection: The Slab Test\n\nTo check if a ray intersects an Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB), we use the **Slab Test**. An AABB is the intersection of three pairs of parallel planes (slabs) aligned with the X, Y, and Z coordinate axes.\n\nFor each axis, we find the entry and exit parameters ($t_{\\text{min}}$ and $t_{\\text{max}}$) where the ray intersects the slab boundaries:\n\n$$t_{\\text{min},x} = \\frac{x_{\\text{min}} - o_x}{d_x}, \\quad t_{\\text{max},x} = \\frac{x_{\\text{max}} - o_x}{d_x}$$\n\n(If $d_x < 0$, we swap $t_{\\text{min},x}$ and $t_{\\text{max},x}$).\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Slabs (Vertical lines representing X plane bounds) -->\n  <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"320\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  \n  <!-- AABB -->\n  <rect x=\"160\" y=\"60\" width=\"160\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.08\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <!-- Ray -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"180\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  \n  <!-- Near and Far intersection parameters -->\n  <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"140\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">t_near</text>\n  \n  <circle cx=\"320\" cy=\"81\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">t_far</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nAfter calculating the intervals for all three axes, the ray encloses the AABB if the largest entry parameter is less than or equal to the smallest exit parameter:\n\n$$t_{\\text{near}} = \\max(t_{\\text{min},x}, t_{\\text{min},y}, t_{\\text{min},z})$$\n$$t_{\\text{far}} = \\min(t_{\\text{max},x}, t_{\\text{max},y}, t_{\\text{max},z})$$\n\nThe ray intersects the AABB if:\n$$t_{\\text{near}} \\le t_{\\text{far}} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad t_{\\text{far}} \\ge 0$$\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Example: Slab Test (Python)\n\n```python\nclass AABB:\n    def __init__(self, min_pt, max_pt):\n        self.min = min_pt # (xmin, ymin, zmin)\n        self.max = max_pt # (xmax, ymax, zmax)\n\ndef ray_intersects_aabb(ray_origin, ray_direction, aabb):\n    t_near = float('-inf')\n    t_far = float('inf')\n    \n    for i in range(3): # Check X, Y, and Z axes\n        o = ray_origin[i]\n        d = ray_direction[i]\n        box_min = aabb.min[i]\n        box_max = aabb.max[i]\n        \n        if abs(d) < 1e-6:\n            # Ray is parallel to this slab; if origin lies outside, no intersection\n            if o < box_min or o > box_max:\n                return None\n        else:\n            # Solve intersection parameters\n            t1 = (box_min - o) / d\n            t2 = (box_max - o) / d\n            \n            # Swap if needed\n            if t1 > t2:\n                t1, t2 = t2, t1\n                \n            # Update overall entry/exit bounds\n            t_near = max(t_near, t1)\n            t_far = min(t_far, t2)\n            \n            # If entry is after exit, the ray misses the box\n            if t_near > t_far:\n                return None\n                \n    # If exit is behind the ray origin, there is no forward intersection\n    if t_far < 0.0:\n        return None\n        \n    return t_near # Returns the intersection distance\n```\n",
  "title": "Ray Intersection Math",
  "tagline": "Ray-Plane, Ray-Sphere quadratic formulas, and AABB Slab Tests.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
  "readTime": 15,
