  "slug": "game-phys-convexity",
  "markdown": "# Game Physics: Geometric Convexity\n\nIn game development and collision detection, shapes are categorized into **Convex** and **Concave**. Understanding convexity is essential because the most efficient physics algorithms (like GJK, EPA, and SAT) are mathematically limited to convex shapes.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Mathematical Definition\n\nA set of points $S$ in coordinate space is **Convex** if, for any two points inside the set, the straight line segment connecting them lies entirely within the set.\n\n$$\\forall \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in S, \\quad \\forall \\lambda \\in [0, 1]: \\quad \\lambda \\mathbf{x} + (1-\\lambda)\\mathbf{y} \\in S$$\n\nIf even a single pair of internal points can be connected by a line segment that passes outside the shape's boundary, the shape is **Concave** (or non-convex).\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Convex Shape (Circle/Oval) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(0, 0)\">\n    <ellipse cx=\"130\" cy=\"100\" rx=\"70\" ry=\"50\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n    <circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n    <circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" />\n    <!-- Segment inside -->\n    <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n    <text x=\"80\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">CONVEX (Segment inside)</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- Concave Shape (Crescent/Pacman) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(260, 0)\">\n    <!-- Pacman path -->\n    <path d=\"M 130 50 A 50 50 0 1 0 130 150 L 100 100 Z\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n    <!-- Two points where connection segment passes outside -->\n    <circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"65\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n    <circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"135\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n    <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">CONCAVE (Segment passes outside)</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Why Convexity is Essential in Physics Engines\n\nPhysics engines rely on convexity to optimize calculations:\n\n### 2.1 Uniqueness of Support Points\nFor a convex shape, finding the furthest point along any direction vector $\\mathbf{d}$ (the support function) is a straightforward optimization problem. The function returns a single, unique boundary vertex. For concave shapes, multiple local maxima can exist, confusing distance trackers.\n\n### 2.2 Simplex and Origin Tracking\nAlgorithms like GJK construct a simplex (like a triangle) inside the Minkowski Difference to search for the origin. This mathematical simplex logic is built on the invariant that the Minkowski Difference of two convex shapes is **always convex**. If either shape is concave, the difference shape can have cavities, causing GJK to miss overlaps or loop infinitely.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Handling Concave Meshes in Games\n\nBecause games require complex concave objects (like hollow rooms, caves, or hollow containers), engines bypass this limitation using two approaches:\n\n1.  **Static Meshes (World Terrain):** Non-moving objects are stored as indexed triangle meshes. The physics engine checks collisions using raycasts directly against individual triangles, which are themselves convex (flat surfaces).\n2.  **Dynamic Meshes (Moving Solids):** Complex moving shapes are split into collections of multiple smaller convex shapes joined together (known as **Convex Decomposition**).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example: Convexity Angle Test (Python)\n\nTo verify if a 2D polygon is convex, we traverse its boundary vertices and check the direction of the cross product for every consecutive edge pair. If all cross products point in the same direction (e.g. all positive), the polygon is convex:\n\n```python\ndef is_convex_polygon(vertices):\n    n = len(vertices)\n    if n < 3:\n        return False\n        \n    sign = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        p1 = vertices[i]\n        p2 = vertices[(i + 1) % n]\n        p3 = vertices[(i + 2) % n]\n        \n        # Calculate cross product of edge 1 (p1->p2) and edge 2 (p2->p3)\n        # Vector 1 = (p2_x - p1_x, p2_y - p1_y)\n        # Vector 2 = (p3_x - p2_x, p3_y - p2_y)\n        cross_product = (p2[0] - p1[0]) * (p3[1] - p2[1]) - (p2[1] - p1[1]) * (p3[0] - p2[0])\n        \n        if cross_product != 0.0:\n            current_sign = 1 if cross_product > 0 else -1\n            if sign == 0:\n                sign = current_sign\n            elif current_sign != sign:\n                # Sign change detected -> polygon has an inward cavity (concave)\n                return False\n                \n    return True\n\n# Test 1: Simple square (Convex)\nsquare = [(0,0), (2,0), (2,2), (0,2)]\nprint(\"Is square convex:\", is_convex_polygon(square)) # Expected: True\n\n# Test 2: Star/Dart shape (Concave)\ndart = [(0,0), (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (0,2)]\nprint(\"Is dart convex:\", is_convex_polygon(dart))     # Expected: False\n```\n",
  "title": "Geometric Convexity",
  "tagline": "Convex sets, line segment tests, and physics solver optimizations.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
