  "slug": "game-phys-concave",
  "markdown": "# Game Physics: Concave Shapes\n\nA **Concave Shape** is any shape that contains internal cavities, indentations, or hollow regions (e.g. donuts, tea cups, caves, or rooms). \n\nWhile concave shapes are common in game environments, they present major computational challenges for physics engines because standard collision algorithms (GJK, EPA, and SAT) assume convexity.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Concave Collision Challenge\n\nWhy can't we run GJK directly on a concave mesh?\n\n1.  **Multiple Support Points:** A search direction vector $\\mathbf{d}$ can yield multiple local maximum dot-products along a cavity, making the support function unstable.\n2.  **Non-Convex Minkowski Difference:** The Minkowski Difference of a convex and a concave shape is itself concave. When GJK builds a simplex inside it, the simplex can clip outside the shape boundary, causing the solver to miss collisions.\n\nTo simulate concave shapes, physics engines use one of three techniques: **Triangle Meshes**, **Convex Decomposition**, or **Composite Colliders**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Triangle Meshes (Static Terrain)\n\nFor static environments (like a landscape or building interiors), engines store the collision shape as an indexed list of flat triangles (**Trimesh**). \n\n*   Since a single flat triangle is convex, the engine resolves collisions by checking the moving entity against individual triangles.\n*   To avoid checking the entity against every triangle in the world, the trimesh is wrapped inside a **Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH)** tree. The engine sweeps down the tree, isolating only the few triangles near the player.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Convex Decomposition\n\nFor dynamic, moving concave bodies (like a hollow chair or a curved boomerang), trimesh solvers are too slow and unstable. Instead, the engine performs **Convex Decomposition**: splitting the concave shape into a compound collection of smaller, intersecting convex shapes.\n\n$$\\text{Shape}_{\\text{concave}} = \\bigcup_{i=1}^{M} \\text{Shape}_{\\text{convex},i}$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Original Concave L-Shape (Left) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(40, 20)\">\n    <path d=\"M 10 20 L 70 20 L 70 100 L 130 100 L 130 140 L 10 140 Z\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Original Concave Shape</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- Arrow -->\n  <path d=\"M 205 100 L 275 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-emerald)\" />\n  <text x=\"215\" y=\"85\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Decompose</text>\n  \n  <!-- Decomposed Convex Pieces (Right) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(300, 20)\">\n    <!-- Piece 1 (Vertical block) -->\n    <rect x=\"10\" y=\"20\" width=\"60\" height=\"120\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"2\" />\n    <text x=\"20\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Piece 1</text>\n    \n    <!-- Piece 2 (Horizontal block - slightly offset for clarity) -->\n    <rect x=\"75\" y=\"100\" width=\"60\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" rx=\"2\" />\n    <text x=\"85\" y=\"125\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Piece 2</text>\n    \n    <text x=\"15\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Compound Convex Union</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nAt runtime, the engine runs GJK and EPA separately on each convex sub-piece, summing the resulting contact points to resolve the overall collision.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python - Triangle Mesh Sweep)\n\nHere is a Python simulator showing how an engine iterates over static mesh triangles to resolve intersections with a player bounding sphere:\n\n```python\nclass Triangle2D:\n    def __init__(self, p1, p2, p3):\n        self.points = [p1, p2, p3]\n        \n    def get_closest_point(self, target):\n        # Finds the closest coordinate on this flat triangle to a target point\n        # (Simplified projection checks for illustrative purposes)\n        min_dist = float('inf')\n        closest = None\n        for i in range(3):\n            a = self.points[i]\n            b = self.points[(i + 1) % 3]\n            # Projection of target on edge segment ab\n            c = get_closest_point_on_segment(a, b, target)\n            dist = distance(c, target)\n            if dist < min_dist:\n                min_dist = dist\n                closest = c\n        return closest\n\nclass StaticMeshCollider:\n    def __init__(self, triangles):\n        self.triangles = triangles # List of Triangle2D objects\n        \n    def solve_sphere_collision(self, sphere_pos, sphere_radius):\n        contacts = []\n        for tri in self.triangles:\n            # 1. Find closest point on this triangle to sphere center\n            cp = tri.get_closest_point(sphere_pos)\n            dist = distance(cp, sphere_pos)\n            \n            # 2. If distance is less than radius, we have a collision\n            if dist < sphere_radius:\n                overlap = sphere_radius - dist\n                # normal points from triangle surface to sphere center\n                normal = normalize_vector(subtract_vectors(sphere_pos, cp))\n                contacts.append((normal, overlap, cp))\n        return contacts # Return contact details to solver\n```\n",
