  "slug": "game-gfx-mapping",
  "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Surface & Tangent Space Mapping\n\nTo display complex surface details (like brick textures, metal scratches, or fabric patterns) without modeling every detail as 3D geometry, we wrap 2D images (textures) around 3D meshes. This process relies on **UV Mapping** and **Tangent Space Coordinate Frames**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. UV Coordinate Mapping\n\nEvery vertex on a 3D mesh is assigned a 2D coordinate $(u, v)$ where:\n*   **$u$** represents the horizontal position on the texture image, normalized from $[0.0, 1.0]$.\n*   **$v$** represents the vertical position, normalized from $[0.0, 1.0]$.\n\nWhen a triangle is rasterized, the GPU interpolates these $(u, v)$ coordinates across the pixels of the triangle. The fragment shader then uses these coordinates to sample color values from the texture.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tangent Space: The TBN Matrix\n\nA 3D coordinate system defined relative to a flat triangle's surface is called **Tangent Space**. We use it to calculate lighting effects (like normal mapping) locally on the triangle's surface.\n\nTangent space is defined by three mutually perpendicular basis vectors:\n*   **Tangent ($\\mathbf{T}$):** Vector pointing parallel to the surface, along the direction of the horizontal texture coordinate $u$.\n*   **Bitangent ($\\mathbf{B}$):** Vector pointing parallel to the surface, along the direction of the vertical texture coordinate $v$.\n*   **Normal ($\\mathbf{N}$):** Vector pointing perpendicular to the surface.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 240\" width=\"100%\" height=\"240\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Triangle surface -->\n  <polygon points=\"100,160 380,180 280,80\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <!-- Basis Origin (Point on surface) -->\n  <circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"140\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Origin</text>\n  \n  <!-- Normal Vector N (Perpendicular) -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  <text x=\"260\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">Normal N</text>\n  \n  <!-- Tangent Vector T (Along U coordinate axis) -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"350\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n  <text x=\"355\" y=\"154\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">Tangent T</text>\n  \n  <!-- Bitangent Vector B (Along V coordinate axis) -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-emerald)\" />\n  <text x=\"135\" y=\"85\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"12\">Bitangent B</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nThese three vectors form the **TBN Matrix** $M_{\\text{TBN}}$:\n\n$$M_{\\text{TBN}} = \\begin{bmatrix} T_x & B_x & N_x \\\\ T_y & B_y & N_y \\\\ T_z & B_z & N_z \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nThis matrix is used to transform vectors (such as light direction vectors) from world space into tangent space, or to transform normal vectors sampled from a normal map back into world space.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Mathematical Derivation of T and B\n\nFor a triangle with vertex positions $\\mathbf{p}_0, \\mathbf{p}_1, \\mathbf{p}_2$ and corresponding texture coordinates $(u_0, v_0), (u_1, v_1), (u_2, v_2)$, we define edge vectors:\n\n$$\\mathbf{e}_1 = \\mathbf{p}_1 - \\mathbf{p}_0, \\quad \\mathbf{e}_2 = \\mathbf{p}_2 - \\mathbf{p}_0$$\n$$\\Delta u_1 = u_1 - u_0, \\quad \\Delta v_1 = v_1 - v_0$$\n$$\\Delta u_2 = u_2 - u_0, \\quad \\Delta v_2 = v_2 - v_0$$\n\nThe edge vectors relate to the Tangent and Bitangent vectors by:\n\n$$\\mathbf{e}_1 = \\Delta u_1 \\mathbf{T} + \\Delta v_1 \\mathbf{B}$$\n$$\\mathbf{e}_2 = \\Delta u_2 \\mathbf{T} + \\Delta v_2 \\mathbf{B}$$\n\nWriting this as a matrix equation and solving for $\\mathbf{T}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ yields:\n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix} \\mathbf{T} \\\\ \\mathbf{B} \\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{1}{\\Delta u_1 \\Delta v_2 - \\Delta u_2 \\Delta v_1} \\begin{bmatrix} \\Delta v_2 & -\\Delta v_1 \\\\ -\\Delta u_2 & \\Delta u_1 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} \\mathbf{e}_1 \\\\ \\mathbf{e}_2 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python - TBN Generator)\n\n```python\nclass TriangleMeshFace:\n    def __init__(self, p0, p1, p2, uv0, uv1, uv2):\n        self.positions = [p0, p1, p2] # Vector coordinates (x,y,z)\n        self.uvs = [uv0, uv1, uv2]     # Texture coordinates (u,v)\n        \n    def calculate_tangent_basis(self):\n        p0, p1, p2 = self.positions\n        uv0, uv1, uv2 = self.uvs\n        \n        # Calculate edge vectors\n        e1 = [p1[i] - p0[i] for i in range(3)]\n        e2 = [p2[i] - p0[i] for i in range(3)]\n        \n        # Calculate coordinate differences\n        du1 = uv1[0] - uv0[0]\n        dv1 = uv1[1] - uv0[1]\n        du2 = uv2[0] - uv0[0]\n        dv2 = uv2[1] - uv0[1]\n        \n        # Calculate determinant coefficient (r = 1.0 / (du1*dv2 - du2*dv1))\n        det = du1 * dv2 - du2 * dv1\n        r = 1.0 / det if abs(det) > 1e-6 else 1.0\n        \n        # Calculate Tangent T\n        tx = r * (dv2 * e1[0] - dv1 * e2[0])\n        ty = r * (dv2 * e1[1] - dv1 * e2[1])\n        tz = r * (dv2 * e1[2] - dv1 * e2[2])\n        tangent = (tx, ty, tz)\n        \n        # Calculate Bitangent B\n        bx = r * (-du2 * e1[0] + du1 * e2[0])\n        by = r * (-du2 * e1[1] + du1 * e2[1])\n        bz = r * (-du2 * e1[2] + du1 * e2[2])\n        bitangent = (bx, by, bz)\n        \n        # Calculate Normal N: N = T x B\n        nx = ty * bz - tz * by\n        ny = tz * bx - tx * bz\n        nz = tx * by - ty * bx\n        normal = (nx, ny, nz)\n        \n        return tangent, bitangent, normal\n```\n",
  "title": "Surface & Tangent Space Mapping",
  "tagline": "Wrapping 2D texture coordinate assets across 3D meshes using tangent spaces and TBN matrices.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
  "readTime": 15,
  "lastUpdated": "July 2026",
