  "slug": "game-gfx-diffuse",
  "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Diffuse Shading Models\n\n**Diffuse Shading** represents the scattering of light from a surface uniformly in all directions. It models rough, matte surfaces (like paper, chalk, or clay) where the perceived brightness does not change with the viewer's position.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Lambert's Cosine Law\n\nThe standard model for diffuse reflection is **Lambertian Shading**. It is based on **Lambert's Cosine Law**, which states that the radiant intensity observed from an ideal diffuse surface is directly proportional to the cosine of the angle $\\theta$ between the surface normal $\\mathbf{N}$ and the light source direction $\\mathbf{L}$:\n\n$$I_{\\text{diffuse}} = I_{\\text{light}} \\cdot \\mathbf{\\rho} \\cdot \\max(\\mathbf{N} \\cdot \\mathbf{L}, 0)$$\n\nWhere $\\mathbf{\\rho}$ is the albedo (base color reflectance of the surface).\n\n### The Projection Dilution Effect:\nWhy does the angle change brightness? When light hits a surface perpendicularly ($\\theta = 0, \\cos(0) = 1$), the light beam covers a minimal area, maximizing its energy density. When the light hits at a shallow angle, the same beam is spread across a larger surface area, diluting its intensity:\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Surface -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Perpendicular Light Beam (Dense) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(40, 0)\">\n    <rect x=\"60\" y=\"30\" width=\"40\" height=\"140\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" />\n    <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n    <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n    <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-amber)\" />\n    <!-- Normal -->\n    <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n    \n    <text x=\"35\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Perpendicular (Dense)</text>\n  </g>\n  \n  <!-- Angled Light Beam (Spread/Diluted) -->\n  <g transform=\"translate(240, 0)\">\n    <!-- Parallel rays projected at angle -->\n    <path d=\"M 40 30 L 100 170 L 210 170 L 150 30 Z\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" />\n    <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n    <line x1=\"150\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n    \n    <!-- Normal -->\n    <line x1=\"155\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"155\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-blue)\" />\n    \n    <text x=\"90\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Angled (Diluted Area)</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Advanced Diffuse Models: Oren-Nayar\n\nLambertian shading assumes a perfectly smooth surface. However, real-world rough materials (like sand, concrete, or fabric) display **retro-reflection**: they reflect more light back in the direction of the light source, making them appear brighter at grazing angles than predicted by Lambert's law.\n\nTo simulate this, physically-based shaders use the **Oren-Nayar model**. It models the surface as a collection of microscopic V-shaped cavities (microfacets):\n\n$$L = \\frac{\\rho}{\\pi} \\cos\\theta_i \\left( A + B \\max(0, \\cos(\\phi_r - \\phi_i)) \\sin\\alpha \\tan\\beta \\right)$$\n\nWhere $A$ and $B$ are constants derived from the surface roughness parameter $\\sigma$. If the roughness is $0$, Oren-Nayar simplifies back to the standard Lambertian model.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Half-Lambert Shading (Optimizing Shadows)\n\nIn real-time games, regions that receive no direct light ($\\mathbf{N} \\cdot \\mathbf{L} \\le 0$) fall instantly to pitch black, which can look unnatural in dynamic environments.\n\nTo create a softer, more stylized look, Valve introduced **Half-Lambert Shading** for *Team Fortress 2*. It scales the dot product from the range $[-1, 1]$ to $[0, 1]$, and squares the result to prevent harsh dark backfaces:\n\n$$I_{\\text{half-lambert}} = \\left( 0.5 (\\mathbf{N} \\cdot \\mathbf{L}) + 0.5 \\right)^2$$\n\nThis wraps lighting around the dark side of objects, simulating indirect bounce light cheaply.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (GLSL Fragment Shader)\n\n```glsl\n#version 330 core\nout vec4 FragColor;\n\nin vec3 FragPos;\nin vec3 Normal;\n\nuniform vec3 lightPos;\nuniform vec3 lightColor;\nuniform vec3 objectColor;\nuniform bool useHalfLambert;\n\nvoid main()\n{\n    vec3 norm = normalize(Normal);\n    vec3 lightDir = normalize(lightPos - FragPos);\n    \n    float diffuseIntensity = 0.0;\n    \n    if (useHalfLambert)\n    {\n        // Half-Lambert: scale and square to soften dark side\n        float cosTheta = dot(norm, lightDir);\n        diffuseIntensity = pow(cosTheta * 0.5 + 0.5, 2.0);\n    }\n    else\n    {\n        // Standard Lambertian Shading\n        diffuseIntensity = max(dot(norm, lightDir), 0.0);\n    }\n    \n    vec3 diffuse = diffuseIntensity * lightColor * objectColor;\n    FragColor = vec4(diffuse, 1.0);\n}\n```\n",
  "title": "Diffuse Shading Models",
  "tagline": "Lambert's Cosine Law projection dilution, Oren-Nayar retro-reflection, and Valve's Half-Lambert shading.",
