  "slug": "game-gfx-bump",
  "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Bump & Normal Mapping\n\nTo make surfaces look detailed and textured (like rough stone, wrinkled skin, or riveted metal plates), we could model every scratch with thousands of polygons. However, this is too slow for real-time rendering. \n\nInstead, we use **Bump Mapping** and **Normal Mapping** to modify how light reflects off a surface, creating the illusion of depth without adding any new geometry.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Bump Mapping vs. Normal Mapping\n\n*   **Bump Mapping (Blinn, 1978):** Uses a grayscale **Height Map** texture. The shader calculates the rate of change (gradients) of the height values across the surface to perturb the surface normal vector. This requires extra calculations at runtime.\n*   **Normal Mapping:** Stores the perturbed 3D normal vectors directly inside an RGB texture (the **Normal Map**). This avoids the gradient calculations, making it much faster and the standard choice for modern GPUs.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Normal Map Encoding and Decoding\n\nA normal vector is a unit vector whose components range from $[-1.0, 1.0]$. However, standard texture color channels (Red, Green, Blue) store values from $[0.0, 1.0]$. \n\nTo store normal vectors inside textures, we **encode** them:\n\n$$\\mathbf{c}_{\\text{texel}} = 0.5 \\mathbf{n}_{\\text{tangent}} + 0.5$$\n\nWhen the GPU samples the normal map in the fragment shader, it reverses this formula to **decode** the vector back into its original coordinates:\n\n$$\\mathbf{n}_{\\text{tangent}} = 2.0 \\mathbf{c}_{\\text{texel}} - 1.0$$\n\n### Why are normal maps predominantly blue?\nIn tangent space, a flat surface has a normal pointing straight up along the local Z-axis: $\\mathbf{n} = (0, 0, 1)$. Encoding this vector:\n\n$$\\mathbf{c}_{\\text{texel}} = 0.5(0, 0, 1) + 0.5 = (0.5, 0.5, 1.0)$$\n\nThis coordinate maps directly to a **lavender-blue** color (Red: 50%, Green: 50%, Blue: 100%). Any perturbations (bumps) create small shifts in the Red (X) and Green (Y) channels.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 180\" width=\"100%\" height=\"180\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Flat geometry surface -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Original normal vectors (pointing straight up) -->\n  <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"380\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <text x=\"35\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Flat Normal</text>\n  \n  <!-- Perturbed Normal vectors from texture -->\n  <!-- Left normal tilted left -->\n  <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  <!-- Center normal straight -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  <!-- Right normal tilted right -->\n  <line x1=\"380\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"410\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"175\" y=\"50\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Perturbed Normals (Shading illusion of depth)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tangent Space Transformation\n\nThe decoded normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_{\\text{tangent}}$ is relative to the triangle's flat local surface. To use this normal in lighting equations, we must transform it into world space. We do this using the **TBN Matrix** calculated from the triangle's vertices and UV coordinates:\n\n$$\\mathbf{n}_{\\text{world}} = M_{\\text{TBN}} \\times \\mathbf{n}_{\\text{tangent}}$$\n\nWhere:\n$$\\mathbf{n}_{\\text{world}} = T \\cdot n_{\\text{tangent},x} + B \\cdot n_{\\text{tangent},y} + N \\cdot n_{\\text{tangent},z}$$\n\nThis transforms the normal vector to match the world's orientation, allowing the shader to calculate reflections and shadows correctly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (GLSL Fragment Shader)\n\n```glsl\n#version 330 core\nout vec4 FragColor;\n\nin vec2 TexCoords;\nin vec3 FragPos;\nin vec3 TangentLightPos;\nin vec3 TangentViewPos;\nin vec3 TangentFragPos;\n\nuniform sampler2D diffuseMap;\nuniform sampler2D normalMap;\n\nvoid main()\n{\n    // 1. Sample and decode normal from normal map into tangent space\n    vec3 normalColor = texture(normalMap, TexCoords).rgb;\n    vec3 normal = normalize(normalColor * 2.0 - 1.0); // Decode: scale and bias\n    \n    // 2. Sample diffuse base color\n    vec3 color = texture(diffuseMap, TexCoords).rgb;\n    \n    // 3. Diffuse lighting in Tangent Space (avoids transforming normal to world space)\n    vec3 lightDir = normalize(TangentLightPos - TangentFragPos);\n    float diff = max(dot(normal, lightDir), 0.0);\n    vec3 diffuse = diff * color;\n    \n    FragColor = vec4(diffuse, 1.0);\n}\n```\n",
  "title": "Bump & Normal Mapping",
  "tagline": "Simulating fine surface depth details by encoding perturbed 3D normal vectors inside RGB texture channels.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
  "readTime": 15,
  "lastUpdated": "July 2026",
  "prerequisites": [],
  "related": [],
