  "slug": "game-gfx-horizon",
  "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Horizon Mapping\n\n**Horizon Mapping** is a texture-based rendering technique introduced by Nelson Max in 1988. It calculates realistic **self-shadowing** on bumpy surfaces without requiring expensive 3D shadow checks or raytracing.\n\nWhile normal mapping perturbs lighting calculations, and parallax mapping offsets texture coordinates, neither can calculate shadows cast by bumps onto other parts of the same surface. Horizon mapping solves this.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Principle & Horizon Angle\n\nFor any point $\\mathbf{p}$ on a bumpy surface, there is an elevation threshold in every direction called the **Horizon Angle ($\\theta_H$)**. If a light source lies below this horizon angle, the point lies in shadow, blocked by an adjacent bump.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Bumpy height profile -->\n  <path d=\"M 50 170 Q 120 170 170 110 Q 220 50 270 120 Q 320 180 390 140 Q 420 120 450 150\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Valley Point p -->\n  <circle cx=\"270\" cy=\"120\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Point p</text>\n  \n  <!-- Horizon Angle Line (tangent to peak at 170,110) -->\n  <line x1=\"270\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  <!-- Angle arc -->\n  <path d=\"M 230 116 A 40 40 0 0 1 270 120\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">θ_H (Horizon Angle)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Light Source below horizon (Shadowed) -->\n  <circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"80\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#27272a\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"110\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"95\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">BLOCKED</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Pre-calculating and Encoding Horizon Data\n\nEvaluating the height field at runtime to find self-shadows is too slow. Instead, the engine pre-calculates the horizon angles for every pixel in a preprocessing step:\n\n1.  For each pixel, we check the height map along a set of radial directions (typically 8 directions spaced at $45^\\circ$ intervals around the circle).\n2.  We calculate the maximum elevation angle $\\theta_H$ in each direction.\n3.  These 8 angles are packed into two 4-channel textures (or a single texture using spherical harmonics).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Shading Check at Runtime\n\nIn the fragment shader:\n1.  Project the light source vector $\\mathbf{L}$ onto the tangent plane to find its direction angle.\n2.  Sample the pre-calculated horizon texture and interpolate the horizon angle $\\theta_H$ for that direction.\n3.  Calculate the light's actual elevation angle:\n    $$\\theta_L = \\arcsin(\\mathbf{L} \\cdot \\mathbf{N})$$\n4.  Compare the angles:\n    *   **$\\theta_L < \\theta_H$:** The light lies below the horizon; the pixel is in shadow. Set diffuse/specular intensity to $0$.\n    *   **$\\theta_L \\ge \\theta_H$:** The light is above the horizon; calculate standard lighting.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python - Horizon Angle Precalculator)\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nclass HeightField:\n    def __init__(self, width, height, data):\n        self.w = width\n        self.h = height\n        self.data = data # 2D array of heights\n        \n    def get_height(self, x, y):\n        # Clamp to bounds\n        cx = max(0, min(int(x), self.w - 1))\n        cy = max(0, min(int(y), self.h - 1))\n        return self.data[cy][cx]\n        \n    def calculate_horizon_angle(self, start_x, start_y, direction_angle, step_size=1.0, max_steps=16):\n        # direction_angle in radians\n        dx = math.cos(direction_angle) * step_size\n        dy = math.sin(direction_angle) * step_size\n        \n        start_height = self.get_height(start_x, start_y)\n        max_slope = 0.0 # tangent of the horizon angle\n        \n        for step in range(1, max_steps + 1):\n            curr_x = start_x + dx * step\n            curr_y = start_y + dy * step\n            \n            curr_height = self.get_height(curr_x, curr_y)\n            height_diff = curr_height - start_height\n            distance = step * step_size\n            \n            if height_diff > 0.0:\n                slope = height_diff / distance\n                max_slope = max(max_slope, slope)\n                \n        # Horizon Angle theta_H = arctan(max_slope)\n        return math.atan(max_slope)\n```\n",
  "title": "Horizon Mapping",
  "tagline": "Precalculating self-shadowing details for bumpy surfaces by measuring direction-based elevation limits.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
  "readTime": 15,
  "lastUpdated": "July 2026",
  "prerequisites": [],
