  "slug": "game-light-source",
  "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Light Sources & Attenuation\n\nIn computer graphics, we simulate the emission of light by defining virtual **Light Sources**. To render realistic environments, we categorize these light sources into different mathematical models and calculate how their light intensity decays over distance (**Attenuation**).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Light Source Models\n\nModern graphics engines define three primary light types:\n\n| Light Type | Emits From | Direction | Attenuation | Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Directional** | Infinite distance | Parallel rays | None (constant) | Sunlight |\n| **Point Light** | Single 3D position | Sphere (all directions) | Decay over distance | Lightbulb |\n| **Spotlight** | Single 3D position | Cone shape | Decay + Angle falloff | Flashlight |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mathematical Modeling of Light Decay (Attenuation)\n\n### 2.1 The Physical Model: Inverse Square Law\nIn the real world, light intensity decays proportionally to the inverse square of the distance ($d$) from the light source. This occurs because the emitted light energy spreads across the surface of an expanding sphere ($A = 4\\pi d^2$):\n\n$$I(d) = \\frac{I_0}{d^2}$$\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Point Light Source -->\n  <circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"110\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" />\n  <text x=\"65\" y=\"145\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Source I0</text>\n  \n  <!-- Expanding rays -->\n  <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Grid 1 (d = 1) -->\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">d = 1</text>\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Area = 1</text>\n  \n  <!-- Grid 2 (d = 2) -->\n  <line x1=\"320\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">d = 2</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Area = 4</text>\n  \n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Intensity scales by 1 / d²</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 The Real-Time Formula\nDirectly applying the formula $1/d^2$ causes numerical issues in games:\n*   When $d \\rightarrow 0$ (e.g. a player walks directly into a light source), the intensity approaches infinity, resulting in blown-out white pixels.\n\nTo avoid this, engines use a **quadratic attenuation formula** to keep the decay controllable:\n\n$$I(d) = \\frac{I_0}{k_c + k_l d + k_q d^2}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   $k_c$ is the **constant term** (usually set to $1.0$, guaranteeing the denominator is never less than $1$, preventing division by zero).\n*   $k_l$ is the **linear term** (smoothly scales decay over distance).\n*   $k_q$ is the **quadratic term** (simulates the rapid, physical decay at close range).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example (GLSL Point Light Calculation)\n\nThis shader implementation computes the distance attenuation of a point light:\n\n```glsl\n#version 330 core\nout vec4 FragColor;\n\nin vec3 FragPos;\nin vec3 Normal;\n\nstruct PointLight {\n    vec3 position;\n    vec3 color;\n    \n    // Attenuation coefficients\n    float constant;\n    float linear;\n    float quadratic;\n};\n\nuniform PointLight light;\nuniform vec3 objectColor;\n\nvoid main()\n{\n    // 1. Calculate vector and distance from fragment to light source\n    vec3 lightVec = light.position - FragPos;\n    float distance = length(lightVec);\n    vec3 lightDir = normalize(lightVec);\n    \n    // 2. Calculate standard diffuse shading\n    vec3 norm = normalize(Normal);\n    float diff = max(dot(norm, lightDir), 0.0);\n    vec3 diffuse = diff * light.color;\n    \n    // 3. Calculate attenuation factor: 1 / (c + l*d + q*d^2)\n    float attenuation = 1.0 / (light.constant + \n                               light.linear * distance + \n                               light.quadratic * (distance * distance));\n                               \n    // 4. Scale lighting by attenuation\n    vec3 finalColor = diffuse * attenuation * objectColor;\n    \n    FragColor = vec4(finalColor, 1.0);\n}\n```\n",
  "title": "Light Sources & Attenuation",
  "tagline": "Simulating point, directional, and spotlights with quadratic attenuation decay math.",
