  "slug": "game-light-2d",
  "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: 2D Lighting & Shadow Extrusion\n\nWhile 3D graphics utilize depth buffers and shadow projection matrices, 2D game engines (such as top-down RPGs or side-scrollers) require specialized techniques to render dynamic lights, soft ambient shadows, and line-of-sight visibility.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Geometry-Based 2D Shadows (Shadow Casters)\n\nTo cast sharp shadows behind 2D objects, engines use **Geometry Extrusion**. When a 2D light source shines on a polygonal obstacle (such as a square block representing a wall):\n\n1.  Identify the vertices of the obstacle.\n2.  Determine which edges face away from the light source (back-faces) and which face toward the light (front-faces).\n3.  For the boundary vertices between front and back edges, construct ray vectors pointing from the light source through the vertices.\n4.  Extrude these vectors to an infinite distance (or to the edge of the screen) to form a **Shadow Volume Polygon**:\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Light source -->\n  <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"110\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"110\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.2\" />\n  <text x=\"85\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Light</text>\n  \n  <!-- Shadow Caster Box -->\n  <rect x=\"240\" y=\"80\" width=\"60\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"245\" y=\"115\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Caster</text>\n  \n  <!-- Boundary Vertices -->\n  <circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"80\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"140\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  \n  <!-- Extrusion vectors -->\n  <!-- Vector 1: Light (100,110) through Vertex A (240, 80) -> dx = 140, dy = -30. Extrude 2x: (380, 50) -->\n  <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"240\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  \n  <!-- Vector 2: Light (100,110) through Vertex B (240, 140) -> dx = 140, dy = 30. Extrude 2x: (380, 170) -->\n  <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <line x1=\"240\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rose)\" />\n  \n  <!-- Shadow Polygon Area -->\n  <polygon points=\"240,80 420,40 420,180 240,140 300,140 300,80\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" />\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"115\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Shadow Polygon</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nThe engine renders these extruded shadow polygons into a stencil buffer or shadow mask texture, blocking light calculations within that area.\n\n---\n\n## 2. 2D Signed Distance Fields (SDFs)\n\nAn alternative to geometry extrusion is **SDF-based lighting**. An SDF is a grid (texture) where each pixel stores the shortest distance to the nearest obstacle. If the pixel is inside the obstacle, the distance is negative.\n\n### 2.1 Raymarching in 2D:\nTo trace shadows:\n1.  In the fragment shader, march a ray from the pixel towards the light source.\n2.  At each step, sample the SDF texture.\n3.  The distance value tells us the maximum safe step size we can take without hitting any obstacle.\n4.  Repeat this process until we either reach the light (unshadowed) or hit an obstacle (shadowed).\n\nThis approach allows for smooth, penumbra-style soft shadows.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example (Python - 2D Shadow Extruder)\n\n```python\nclass Vector2D:\n    def __init__(self, x=0.0, y=0.0):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n\ndef extrude_shadow_vertices(light_pos, vertex_a, vertex_b, extrusion_distance=1000.0):\n    # Calculate direction vectors from light to boundary vertices\n    dir_a_x = vertex_a.x - light_pos.x\n    dir_a_y = vertex_a.y - light_pos.y\n    \n    dir_b_x = vertex_b.x - light_pos.x\n    dir_b_y = vertex_b.y - light_pos.y\n    \n    # Normalize vectors\n    len_a = math.sqrt(dir_a_x**2 + dir_a_y**2)\n    len_b = math.sqrt(dir_b_x**2 + dir_b_y**2)\n    \n    n_a_x = (dir_a_x / len_a) if len_a > 0.0 else 0.0\n    n_a_y = (dir_a_y / len_a) if len_a > 0.0 else 0.0\n    \n    n_b_x = (dir_b_x / len_b) if len_b > 0.0 else 0.0\n    n_b_y = (dir_b_y / len_b) if len_b > 0.0 else 0.0\n    \n    # Extrude vertices\n    extruded_a = Vector2D(\n        vertex_a.x + n_a_x * extrusion_distance,\n        vertex_a.y + n_a_y * extrusion_distance\n    )\n    \n    extruded_b = Vector2D(\n        vertex_b.x + n_b_x * extrusion_distance,\n        vertex_b.y + n_b_y * extrusion_distance\n    )\n    \n    # Return 4 vertices defining the 2D shadow quad\n    return [vertex_a, extruded_a, extruded_b, vertex_b]\n```\n",
  "title": "2D Lighting & Shadow Extrusion",
  "tagline": "Extruding polygonal shadow quads and raymarching 2D Signed Distance Fields (SDFs).",
  "category": "Game Developer",
  "readTime": 15,
  "lastUpdated": "July 2026",
