  "slug": "game-light-stencil-shadow",
  "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Stencil Shadow Volumes\n\n**Stencil Shadow Volumes** is a geometric shadow technique introduced by Franklin Crow in 1977 and popularized by id Software in *Doom 3*. Unlike shadow mapping, which samples a depth texture, shadow volumes use the **Stencil Buffer** to compute pixel-accurate shadow boundaries without aliasing.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Silhouette Extrusion\n\nTo cast shadows using this technique, the engine must construct a **Shadow Volume Mesh** for each shadow caster:\n\n1.  **Silhouette Detection:** Identify the silhouette edges of the mesh relative to the light source. A silhouette edge is shared by one triangle facing the light ($\\mathbf{N}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{L} > 0$) and one facing away ($\\mathbf{N}_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{L} \\le 0$).\n2.  **Extrusion:** Extrude the silhouette edges away from the light source to form the \"walls\" of the shadow volume.\n3.  **Caps:** Add a front cap (the original geometry) and a back cap (projected at infinity) to form a closed 3D volume.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 240\" width=\"100%\" height=\"240\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Light source -->\n  <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"50\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"50\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" />\n  <text x=\"85\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Light</text>\n  \n  <!-- Caster Object -->\n  <polygon points=\"200,90 260,60 280,120 220,130\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Object</text>\n  \n  <!-- Silhouette Vertices -->\n  <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"90\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"120\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  \n  <!-- Extrusion Walls -->\n  <!-- Vector 1: Light (100,50) -> V1 (200, 90) -> Extrude to (380, 162) -->\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"178\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <!-- Vector 2: Light (100,50) -> V2 (280, 120) -> Extrude to (420, 175) -->\n  <line x1=\"280\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  \n  <!-- Shadow Volume Polygon -->\n  <polygon points=\"200,90 420,178 420,175 280,120\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" />\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Shadow Volume</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Stencil Buffer Algorithms\n\nOnce the volume is constructed, we render it into the stencil buffer to tag shadowed pixels.\n\n### 2.1 The Z-Pass Algorithm\n1.  Render the scene's color and depth values normally.\n2.  Disable writing to the color and depth buffers (leaving only the stencil buffer active).\n3.  Set the stencil operation to:\n    *   **Increment** on rendering front-facing shadow volume polygons.\n    *   **Decrement** on rendering back-facing shadow volume polygons.\n4.  If a pixel's final stencil value is non-zero, it lies inside the shadow volume and is in shadow.\n\n*   **Problem:** If the player's camera enters the shadow volume, the Z-pass count fails, causing shadows to invert.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Carmack's Reverse (Z-Fail Algorithm)\nTo solve the camera-in-volume problem, John Carmack proposed checking depth failures instead of passes:\n\n1.  Disable color/depth writing.\n2.  Render the shadow volume, but set stencil operations to trigger when the **Depth Test Fails** (the volume is behind visible geometry):\n    *   **Decrement** when rendering front-facing volume polygons.\n    *   **Increment** when rendering back-facing volume polygons.\n3.  This correctly tags shadowed regions regardless of camera position.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example (Python - Silhouette Edge Finder)\n\n```python\nclass MeshEdge:\n    def __init__(self, v0_idx, v1_idx):\n        self.v0 = min(v0_idx, v1_idx)\n        self.v1 = max(v0_idx, v1_idx)\n        self.faces = [] # Store adjacent face indices (should be 1 or 2)\n\ndef find_silhouette_edges(vertices, faces, light_pos):\n    # faces: list of tuples (v0, v1, v2)\n    # light_pos: 3D vector\n    \n    # 1. Calculate face normal directions relative to the light\n    face_lit = []\n    for f in faces:\n        p0, p1, p2 = vertices[f[0]], vertices[f[1]], vertices[f[2]]\n        \n        # Calculate normal vector\n        ux, uy, uz = p1[0]-p0[0], p1[1]-p0[1], p1[2]-p0[2]\n        vx, vy, vz = p2[0]-p0[0], p2[1]-p0[1], p2[2]-p0[2]\n        nx = uy*vz - uz*vy\n        ny = uz*vx - ux*vz\n        nz = ux*vy - uy*vx\n        \n        # Vector from face center to light source\n        cx = (p0[0] + p1[0] + p2[0]) / 3.0\n        cy = (p0[1] + p1[1] + p2[1]) / 3.0\n        cz = (p0[2] + p1[2] + p2[2]) / 3.0\n        lx, ly, lz = light_pos[0]-cx, light_pos[1]-cy, light_pos[2]-cz\n        \n        # Face faces light if dot product is positive\n        is_lit = (nx*lx + ny*ly + nz*lz) > 0.0\n        face_lit.append(is_lit)\n        \n    # 2. Build unique edge registry\n    edges = {}\n    for f_idx, f in enumerate(faces):\n        for pair in [(f[0], f[1]), (f[1], f[2]), (f[2], f[0])]:\n            edge_key = (min(pair), max(pair))\n            if edge_key not in edges:\n                edges[edge_key] = []\n            edges[edge_key].append(f_idx)\n            \n    # 3. Filter edges that separate a lit face from an unlit face\n    silhouette = []\n    for edge_key, adjacent_faces in edges.items():\n        if len(adjacent_faces) == 2:\n            f0, f1 = adjacent_faces\n            # If one face is lit and the other is dark, it's a silhouette edge\n            if face_lit[f0] != face_lit[f1]:\n                silhouette.append(edge_key)\n                \n    return silhouette\n```\n",
  "title": "Stencil Shadow Volumes",
  "tagline": "Extruding silhouette edges, stencil count passes, and Carmack's Reverse depth-fail checks.",
