  "slug": "game-vis-clipping",
  "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: View Frustum Clipping\n\n**View Frustum Clipping** is the process of cutting geometric primitives (like triangles or lines) that intersect the boundaries of the camera's view volume (frustum). This ensures that the GPU only processes geometry located inside the viewable area, preventing rendering errors.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why Clip in Homogeneous Space?\n\nYou might assume that clipping is done in 2D screen space after the 3D geometry has been projected. However, clipping must be performed in **Homogeneous Clip Space** (before dividing by $w$):\n\n*   **The Singularity Problem:** If a vertex lies behind the camera ($z \\le 0$), dividing its coordinates by a negative $w$ reverses its signs. This projects the vertex onto the screen incorrectly, flipping the geometry backward and creating visual distortion (singularities).\n*   **The Bound Check:** In clip space, a point $(x, y, z, w)$ lies inside the view frustum if:\n    $$-w \\le x \\le w, \\quad -w \\le y \\le w, \\quad 0 \\le z \\le w \\quad (\\text{assuming standard DirectX/Vulkan } Z)$$\n\nCliving geometry in clip space prevents division by zero or negative values during projection.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Sutherland-Hodgman Algorithm\n\nTo clip a triangle against the frustum, the GPU tests the triangle against each of the **six clipping planes** (Left, Right, Bottom, Top, Near, Far) one by one.\n\nFor each plane, we clip the polygon edges using the **Sutherland-Hodgman algorithm**:\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Clipping boundary plane -->\n  <line x1=\"250\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  <text x=\"260\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Clipping Plane</text>\n  \n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"205\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">IN (Visible)</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"205\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">OUT (Clipped)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Intersecting Triangle -->\n  <!-- Vertices: A (130, 70) inside, B (380, 110) outside, C (180, 160) inside -->\n  <polygon points=\"130,70 380,110 180,160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  \n  <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"70\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">A</text>\n  \n  <circle cx=\"380\" cy=\"110\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  <text x=\"390\" y=\"115\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">B (Discard)</text>\n  \n  <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"160\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"170\" y=\"175\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">C</text>\n  \n  <!-- Intersection points generated -->\n  <!-- Intersection on AB at x=250: y ~ 89 -->\n  <circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"89\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">I1</text>\n  \n  <!-- Intersection on BC at x=250: y ~ 142 -->\n  <circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"142\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"158\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">I2</text>\n  \n  <!-- Clipped output polygon (Quad A-I1-I2-C) -->\n  <polygon points=\"130,70 250,89 250,142 180,160\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n*   **Case 1: Both vertices inside:** Add destination vertex to list.\n*   **Case 2: First vertex inside, second outside:** Calculate intersection point $I$ and add $I$ to list. Discard the outside vertex.\n*   **Case 3: Both vertices outside:** Discard both.\n*   **Case 4: First vertex outside, second inside:** Calculate intersection point $I$. Add $I$ and destination vertex to list.\n\nAs shown above, clipping an intersecting triangle against a plane can split it into a quad, which the GPU then triangulates into two new triangles before rasterization.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Intersection Formula\n\nFor an edge connecting vertex $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that intersects a clipping plane, we calculate the intersection point $\\mathbf{I}$:\n\n$$\\mathbf{I} = \\mathbf{a} + t(\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a})$$\n\nThe interpolation factor $t \\in [0, 1]$ represents the ratio of distance:\n\n$$t = \\frac{\\mathbf{d}_p \\cdot \\mathbf{a} + d_{\\text{offset}}}{\\mathbf{d}_p \\cdot (\\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{b})}$$\n\nWhere $\\mathbf{d}_p$ is the normal vector of the clipping plane and $d_{\\text{offset}}$ is its displacement from the origin.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (Python - 1D Edge Clipper)\n\n```python\nclass Vertex3D:\n    def __init__(self, x=0.0, y=0.0, z=0.0):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n        self.z = z\n\ndef clip_edge_plane(vertex_a, vertex_b, plane_z_limit):\n    # Clips an edge against a simple plane: z = plane_z_limit\n    # Assuming plane normal points along positive Z direction\n    dist_a = vertex_a.z - plane_z_limit\n    dist_b = vertex_b.z - plane_z_limit\n    \n    # Check if vertices lie on opposite sides of the plane\n    if dist_a * dist_b < 0.0:\n        # Interpolation factor t\n        t = dist_a / (dist_a - dist_b)\n        \n        # Calculate intersection point coordinates\n        inter_x = vertex_a.x + t * (vertex_b.x - vertex_a.x)\n        inter_y = vertex_a.y + t * (vertex_b.y - vertex_a.y)\n        inter_z = plane_z_limit\n        \n        return Vertex3D(inter_x, inter_y, inter_z)\n        \n    return None # Edge does not cross plane boundary\n```\n",
  "title": "View Frustum Clipping",
  "tagline": "Cutting mesh geometry inside homogeneous clip space using the Sutherland-Hodgman algorithm.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
  "readTime": 15,
  "lastUpdated": "July 2026",
