  "slug": "game-vis-fog",
  "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Fog & Atmospheric Scattering\n\nTo simulate realistic distance depth and scale in virtual environments, we model how light is scattered and absorbed by particles in the air (such as dust, haze, or water droplets). In graphics, this is represented by **Fog** and **Atmospheric Scattering**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Mathematical Fog Decay Models\n\nFog works by blending an object's shaded color $C_{\\text{object}}$ with a constant background fog color $C_{\\text{fog}}$ based on a **Fog Factor ($f$)** that decays from $1.0$ (no fog) to $0.0$ (fully fogged) over distance ($d$):\n\n$$C_{\\text{final}} = f \\cdot C_{\\text{object}} + (1 - f) \\cdot C_{\\text{fog}}$$\n\nWe calculate the fog factor $f$ using three primary equations:\n\n| Model | Formula | Characteristics |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Linear** | $f = \\frac{d_{\\text{end}} - d}{d_{\\text{end}} - d_{\\text{start}}}$ | Simple threshold bounds. Looks artificial. |\n| **Exponential** | $f = e^{-d \\cdot g}$ | Natural density decay. $g$ is the fog density. |\n| **Exp Squared** | $f = e^{-(d \\cdot g)^2}$ | Thicker close-up visibility, decaying rapidly. |\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Grid Axes -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"25\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Visibility Factor f (1.0 to 0.0)</text>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"175\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Distance d</text>\n  \n  <!-- Linear Decay (Straight line) -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Linear Decay</text>\n  \n  <!-- Exponential Decay -->\n  <path d=\"M 60 40 Q 150 140 440 160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Exponential (e^-d)</text>\n\n  <!-- Exponential Squared Decay -->\n  <path d=\"M 60 40 Q 110 50 200 130 T 440 160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\" />\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Exp Squared (e^-d²)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Rayleigh and Mie Scattering\n\nFor outdoor environments, we use **Atmospheric Scattering** models to simulate realistic skies, sunsets, and distant horizons:\n\n1.  **Rayleigh Scattering:** Occurs when light hits particles (like air molecules) that are much smaller than the light's wavelength. It scatters shorter wavelengths (Blue) significantly more than longer ones (Red). This is why the sky appears blue during the day, and turns orange/red at sunset when sunlight travels through a thicker layer of atmosphere.\n    $$\\text{Rayleigh Scattering Coefficient } \\propto \\frac{1}{\\lambda^4}$$\n2.  **Mie Scattering:** Occurs when light hits particles (like dust, smoke, or water droplets) that are roughly equal in size to the light's wavelength. It scatters all wavelengths uniformly, creating a grayish-white haze around the sun or in foggy weather.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example (GLSL Fragment Shader Fog)\n\n```glsl\n#version 330 core\nout vec4 FragColor;\n\nin vec3 FragPos;\nin vec3 Normal;\n\nuniform vec3 cameraPos;\nuniform vec3 objectColor;\nuniform vec3 fogColor;\nuniform float fogDensity;\n\nvoid main()\n{\n    // 1. Calculate distance from camera to fragment\n    float distance = length(cameraPos - FragPos);\n    \n    // 2. Simple lighting calculations (diffuse)\n    vec3 resultColor = objectColor * vec3(0.8); // Simple base ambient/diffuse light\n    \n    // 3. Calculate Exponential Squared Fog Factor: f = e^(- (d * g)^2)\n    float g = fogDensity;\n    float fogFactor = exp(-pow(distance * g, 2.0));\n    fogFactor = clamp(fogFactor, 0.0, 1.0);\n    \n    // 4. Blend final color: f * C_obj + (1-f) * C_fog\n    vec3 finalColor = mix(fogColor, resultColor, fogFactor);\n    \n    FragColor = vec4(finalColor, 1.0);\n}\n```\n",
  "title": "Fog & Atmospheric Scattering",
  "tagline": "Linear and exponential distance visibility decays, Rayleigh blue skies, and Mie grey haze.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
