  "slug": "game-vis-polygon",
  "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Polygon Clipping Algorithms\n\n**Polygon Clipping** is the process of trimming a polygon geometry against a clipping window or boundary. While view frustum clipping operates on triangles in the GPU pipeline, general polygon clipping is used in 2D UI layouts, map rendering, and boolean solid modeling operations (CSG).\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Sutherland-Hodgman Limits\n\nThe **Sutherland-Hodgman algorithm** clips a subject polygon against a convex clipping region by clipping the polygon against each boundary line sequentially. \n\nWhile efficient, it has a core limitation: **it is designed for convex clipping windows and convex polygons**. \n\nIf the subject polygon is concave, Sutherland-Hodgman can generate unwanted connecting lines (\"bridges\") across the empty space between separated visible parts:\n\n```\n    Clipped Concave Shape:\n     /\\       /\\\n    /  \\_____/  \\   <── Extraneous edge connecting separate parts\n   /_____________\\\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Weiler-Atherton Clipping Algorithm\n\nTo handle concave subject polygons and concave clipping windows, we use the **Weiler-Atherton Algorithm** (1977). Instead of simply cutting lines, this algorithm structures the vertices of both polygons as circular list graphs and tracks direction intersections:\n\n1.  **Find Intersections:** Calculate all intersection points between the edges of the subject polygon $S$ and the clip polygon $C$. Tag them as **Entering** or **Leaving** points (relative to the clip polygon's interior).\n2.  **Construct Lists:** Build two vertex lists containing original vertices and intersection points in clockwise order: $L_S$ (Subject) and $L_C$ (Clip).\n3.  **Traverse lists:**\n    *   Start at an unvisited **Entering** intersection point in $L_S$.\n    *   Traverse clockwise along $L_S$ until a **Leaving** intersection point is reached.\n    *   Switch to $L_C$ at that intersection, and traverse clockwise along $L_C$ until the starting Entering point is reached.\n    *   Close the polygon loop. Repeat for any remaining unvisited entering points.\n\nThis graph-switching technique extracts separate, clean polygons without false connecting lines:\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Clip window (Gray Box) -->\n  <rect x=\"150\" y=\"50\" width=\"200\" height=\"120\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"40\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Clip Window</text>\n  \n  <!-- Concave Subject Polygon (Star-like or V-like) -->\n  <!-- Vertices: (100, 110) -> (200, 80) -> (250, 140) -> (300, 80) -> (400, 110) -> (250, 190) -->\n  <polygon points=\"100,110 200,80 250,140 300,80 400,110 250,190\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  \n  <!-- Intersections -->\n  <!-- Left Entering (150, 95) -->\n  <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"95\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"115\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Entering 1</text>\n  \n  <!-- Valley Leaving (220, 110) -->\n  <circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"110\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  \n  <!-- Valley Entering (280, 110) -->\n  <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"110\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  \n  <!-- Right Leaving (350, 95) -->\n  <circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"95\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  <text x=\"355\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Leaving 2</text>\n  \n  <!-- Clipped separated components (Green fill) -->\n  <!-- Component 1 -->\n  <polygon points=\"150,95 200,80 220,110 150,110\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <!-- Component 2 -->\n  <polygon points=\"280,110 300,80 350,95 350,110\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  \n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"205\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Clipped Output: Two separated shapes</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example (Python - Intersection Point Detector)\n\n```python\nclass Point2D:\n    def __init__(self, x=0.0, y=0.0):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n\ndef calculate_line_intersection(p1, p2, p3, p4):\n    # Returns the intersection point of line segment p1-p2 with segment p3-p4\n    # Denominator = (p1.x - p2.x)*(p3.y - p4.y) - (p1.y - p2.y)*(p3.x - p4.x)\n    denom = (p1.x - p2.x) * (p3.y - p4.y) - (p1.y - p2.y) * (p3.x - p4.x)\n    \n    if abs(denom) < 1e-6:\n        return None # Lines are parallel\n        \n    t = ((p1.x - p3.x) * (p3.y - p4.y) - (p1.y - p3.y) * (p3.x - p4.x)) / denom\n    u = -((p1.x - p2.x) * (p1.y - p3.y) - (p1.y - p2.y) * (p1.x - p3.x)) / denom\n    \n    # Check if intersection point lies within both line segments\n    if 0.0 <= t <= 1.0 and 0.0 <= u <= 1.0:\n        inter_x = p1.x + t * (p2.x - p1.x)\n        inter_y = p1.y + t * (p2.y - p1.y)\n        return Point2D(inter_x, inter_y)\n        \n    return None # Segments do not intersect\n```\n",
  "title": "Polygon Clipping Algorithms",
  "tagline": "Sutherland-Hodgman convex limitations and Weiler-Atherton concave graph-switching traversals.",
