  "slug": "game-vis-polyhedron",
  "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Polyhedral Visibility & Portal Rendering\n\nIn computational geometry and graphics, **Polyhedral Visibility** determines the visibility relationships between arbitrary 3D polyhedra. In games, this is most commonly implemented as **Cell-Portal Rendering** to optimize indoor levels (such as dungeon corridors or office buildings).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Cell-Portal Rendering Architecture\n\nCell-Portal systems divide indoor game worlds into distinct spaces connected by visible openings:\n\n*   **Cells:** Individual rooms or corridors (convex or simple concave polyhedra containing walls and objects).\n*   **Portals:** 2D polygons representing openings between adjacent cells (such as doorways, windows, or archways).\n\n### The Recursive Culling Algorithm:\n1.  **Locate Camera:** Determine which cell the player's camera is currently inside (the active cell). Render all static geometry inside this cell.\n2.  **Portal Check:** For each portal in the active cell, check if it intersects the camera's view frustum.\n3.  **Frustum Clipping:** If a portal is visible, construct a new, narrower **Clipped Frustum** using the portal's boundary edges as the new culling planes.\n4.  **Recursive Render:** Travel through the portal to the adjacent cell, culling and rendering its geometry using the new clipped frustum. Repeat this process recursively.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Cell A Boundary -->\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"40\" width=\"150\" height=\"140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"45\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Cell A (Active)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Camera in Cell A -->\n  <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"110\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"45\" y=\"135\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Camera</text>\n  \n  <!-- Portal (Opening between Cell A and Cell B) -->\n  <!-- Located at x = 180, from y=90 to y=130 -->\n  <line x1=\"180\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"4\" />\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Portal</text>\n  \n  <!-- Cell B Boundary -->\n  <rect x=\"180\" y=\"40\" width=\"150\" height=\"140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"195\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Cell B</text>\n  \n  <!-- Clipped Frustum through Portal -->\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"65\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <path d=\"M 180 90 L 330 65 V 155 L 180 130 Z\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" fill-opacity=\"0.05\" />\n  \n  <!-- Object in Cell B inside clipped frustum (Rendered) -->\n  <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"110\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"260\" y=\"135\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Rendered</text>\n  \n  <!-- Object in Cell B outside clipped frustum (Culled) -->\n  <circle cx=\"230\" cy=\"60\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n  <text x=\"215\" y=\"85\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Culled</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nPortal rendering is extremely efficient because the engine completely skips culling and rendering calculations for any cells that do not share a visible line-of-sight portal, making it ideal for large maze-like indoor levels.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Aspect Graphs\n\nFor general polyhedral visibility, computational geometry uses **Aspect Graphs**. \n\nAn aspect graph partitions the space surrounding a 3D polyhedron into cell regions. Within each region, the topological view (the structure of vertices, edges, and visible faces) remains identical. Crossing a boundary line into a new cell changes the \"aspect\" (occlusion status of faces) abruptly.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation Example (Python - Portal Visibility Test)\n\n```python\nclass Portal2D:\n    def __init__(self, y_min, y_max):\n        self.ymin = y_min\n        self.ymax = y_max\n        self.x = 10.0 # Vertical portal plane along X axis\n\nclass Frustum2D:\n    def __init__(self, y_min_bound, y_max_bound):\n        self.ymin = y_min_bound\n        self.ymax = y_max_bound\n\ndef clip_frustum_to_portal(camera_pos, frustum, portal):\n    # Calculates a new, narrower frustum clipped to the portal boundaries\n    # Calculate angles from camera to portal endpoints\n    dy_min = portal.ymin - camera_pos.y\n    dy_max = portal.ymax - camera_pos.y\n    dx = portal.x - camera_pos.x\n    \n    if dx <= 0.0:\n        return None # Portal is behind camera or invalid\n        \n    portal_slope_min = dy_min / dx\n    portal_slope_max = dy_max / dx\n    \n    # Clip slope limits against camera's existing frustum boundaries\n    new_slope_min = max(frustum.ymin, portal_slope_min)\n    new_slope_max = min(frustum.ymax, portal_slope_max)\n    \n    if new_slope_min >= new_slope_max:\n        return None # Portal is completely outside active frustum\n        \n    return Frustum2D(new_slope_min, new_slope_max)\n```\n",
  "title": "Polyhedral Visibility & Portal Rendering",
  "tagline": "Cell-portal rendering architectures, portal frustum clipping, and aspect graphs.",
  "category": "Game Developer",
