  "slug": "game-ai-fuzzy",
  "markdown": "# Game AI: Fuzzy Logic Systems\n\nIn traditional game programming, conditional checks are binary: a player is either *Inside the Danger Zone* (True) or *Outside the Danger Zone* (False). In contrast, **Fuzzy Logic** handles degrees of truth (floating-point values between $0.0$ and $1.0$), simulating human-like decision-making under uncertainty.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Fuzzy Control Loop\n\nA fuzzy logic control system executes in three sequential stages:\n\n```\n[Crisp Input: 12 meters] ──> Fuzzification ──> Rule Inference ──> Defuzzification ──> [Crisp Output: 65% Threat]\n```\n\n### 1.1 Fuzzification\nFuzzification translates a raw, numeric input value (a **crisp input**) into a degree of membership in one or more qualitative ranges (**fuzzy sets**). This mapping is defined by a **Membership Function**:\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Grid Axes -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"50\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <text x=\"35\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Membership μ</text>\n  <text x=\"35\" y=\"175\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">0m</text>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"175\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">50m</text>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"175\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">Distance (Crisp)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Fuzzy Set: Near (Trapezoid) -->\n  <path d=\"M 50 50 L 150 50 L 250 160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#f43f5e\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">NEAR</text>\n  \n  <!-- Fuzzy Set: Medium (Triangle) -->\n  <path d=\"M 150 160 L 250 50 L 350 160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">MEDIUM</text>\n\n  <!-- Fuzzy Set: Far -->\n  <path d=\"M 250 160 L 350 50 L 450 50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n  <text x=\"375\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"10\">FAR</text>\n  \n  <!-- Example: Input x = 200m (which is 0.5 Near and 0.5 Medium) -->\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#a1a1aa\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\" />\n  <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"105\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"9\">x = 15m (Input)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\nAs shown above, a distance of 15m is not classified exclusively. Instead, it is evaluated as:\n*   $\\mu_{\\text{Near}}(15) = 0.5$ (50% member of \"Near\")\n*   $\\mu_{\\text{Medium}}(15) = 0.5$ (50% member of \"Medium\")\n\n---\n\n### 1.2 Fuzzy Logical Operators (Inference rules)\nOnce inputs are fuzzified, we evaluate behavior rules using fuzzy set mathematics:\n\n*   **Fuzzy AND (Intersection):** Selects the minimum value:\n    $$\\mu_{A \\cap B} = \\min(\\mu_A, \\mu_B)$$\n*   **Fuzzy OR (Union):** Selects the maximum value:\n    $$\\mu_{A \\cup B} = \\max(\\mu_A, \\mu_B)$$\n*   **Fuzzy NOT (Complement):**\n    $$\\mu_{\\neg A} = 1.0 - \\mu_A$$\n\n*Example Rule:* `IF (Distance is Near) AND (EnemyCount is High) THEN (Threat is High)`\n$$\\mu_{\\text{ThreatHigh}} = \\min(\\mu_{\\text{DistanceNear}}, \\mu_{\\text{EnemyHigh}})$$\n\n---\n\n### 1.3 Defuzzification\nTo map the evaluated rules back to a physical action (like a steering angle or threat level percentage), we blend the active fuzzy set shapes together. The most common defuzzification method is finding the **Center of Gravity (Centroid)**:\n\n$$\\text{Crisp Output} = \\frac{\\sum (x \\cdot \\mu(x))}{\\sum \\mu(x)}$$\n\n---\n\n## 2. Implementation Example (Python - Simple Linear Fuzzifier)\n\n```python\nclass FuzzySet:\n    def __init__(self, name, a, b, c, d):\n        # Trapezoidal membership boundaries: a, b flat high, c, d flat low\n        self.name = name\n        self.a = float(a)\n        self.b = float(b)\n        self.c = float(c)\n        self.d = float(d)\n        \n    def get_membership(self, x):\n        # Standard trapezoidal membership formula\n        if x <= self.a or x >= self.d:\n            return 0.0\n        elif self.b <= x <= self.c:\n            return 1.0\n        elif self.a < x < self.b:\n            return (x - self.a) / (self.b - self.a)\n        else: # c < x < d\n            return (self.d - x) / (self.d - self.c)\n\n# --- Test Fuzzification ---\nnear_set = FuzzySet(\"NEAR\", 0, 0, 10, 25) # 1.0 from 0 to 10, decays to 0 at 25\n\ndistance = 15.0\nmembership_near = near_set.get_membership(distance)\nprint(f\"Distance {distance}m has membership {membership_near} in set {near_set.name}\")\n# Output: 0.666 (Two-thirds Near)\n```\n",
  "title": "Game AI: Fuzzy Logic Systems",
  "tagline": "Continuous degrees of truth, fuzzification membership graphs, and centroid defuzzification.",
